(window.SLV_PACKS = window.SLV_PACKS || []).push({"id":"access","name":"Access control","blurb":"Card readers, locks, door hardware, and egress rules","calculators":["lockpsu","reader","drop","gauge"],"openTips":10,"draft":false,"verifyOpen":0,"learn":{"modules":[{"num":1,"title":"Foundations","introHtml":"<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> techs new to access control, including intrusion and fire techs crossing over. <strong>You'll be able to:</strong> name every part of an access-controlled door, explain fail-safe and fail-secure, describe the power and wiring an access door uses, and work on doors without trapping or locking out anyone.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"1.1","title":"What access control does","html":"<p>An access control system decides <strong>who</strong> can go through <strong>which door</strong> at <strong>what time</strong>, and keeps a record of every attempt.</p>\n<p>It replaces keys with credentials (cards, fobs, phones, PINs) that can be added, removed, or limited from software. When an employee leaves, you delete their card instead of rekeying the building.</p>\n<p>Three jobs, all at once:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Keep people out</strong> who shouldn't be there (the secure side of the door).</li><li><strong>Let people out</strong> freely, every time, even when the power or the system fails (the egress side of the door). This is the job that's regulated by building and life safety codes.</li><li><strong>Report</strong> what happened: who badged in, which doors were forced or propped open, which doors are offline.</li></ul>\n<p>Access control isn't a burglar alarm, but the two often work together. The access system may arm and disarm the intrusion panel, and door alarms often report to the same monitoring center.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.2","title":"Parts of an access-controlled door","html":"<p>Almost every access door has the same building blocks.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Credential</strong></td><td>Card, fob, phone, or PIN that identifies the person</td><td>In the user's pocket</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reader</strong></td><td>Reads the credential and sends the number to the controller</td><td>Secure side, next to the door</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Controller</strong></td><td>Decides yes or no, drives the lock, logs the event</td><td>Secure area: IT closet, above ceiling, or at the door (edge controller)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Electrified lock</strong></td><td>Electric strike, magnetic lock, electrified lockset, or electrified exit device</td><td>In or on the door and frame</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Door position switch (DPS)</strong></td><td>Tells the controller whether the door is closed</td><td>Top of the door frame, usually recessed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Request to exit (REX)</strong></td><td>Tells the controller someone is leaving, so opening the door isn't a forced-door alarm</td><td>Egress side: motion sensor above the door, or a switch in the exit hardware</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Power supply</strong></td><td>Powers the locks (and often the controller and readers) with battery backup</td><td>Next to the controller</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Software</strong></td><td>Where cardholders, access levels, schedules, and reports live</td><td>Server, workstation, or cloud</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>           SECURE SIDE             |            EGRESS SIDE\n                                   |\n   [READER]        [DPS]===========|  [REX motion]\n      |              |   DOOR      |\n      |              |  [LOCK]     |  [lever / exit device]\n      |              |             |\n   [CONTROLLER] ---- [POWER SUPPLY] ---- fire alarm release</code></pre></div>\n<p>You'll learn each part in detail in Modules 3, 4, and 5.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.3","title":"Fail-safe and fail-secure","html":"<p>These two words come up on every door. They describe what the lock does <strong>on the secure side when it loses power</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Power off</th><th>Power on</th><th>Typical devices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fail-safe</strong></td><td><strong>Unlocked</strong></td><td>Locked</td><td>Magnetic locks; some electric strikes and locksets set fail-safe</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fail-secure</strong></td><td><strong>Locked</strong></td><td>Unlocked</td><td>Most electric strikes; most electrified locksets and exit devices</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Memory aid: fail-<strong>safe</strong> keeps <strong>people</strong> safe (door opens); fail-<strong>secure</strong> keeps the <strong>building</strong> secure (door stays locked).</p>\n<p><strong>The egress side is always free.</strong> On a properly installed door, someone inside can always get out with one motion, whatever the lock does on the secure side. A fail-secure strike stays locked from the outside during a power failure, but the lever or panic bar on the inside still opens the door mechanically. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></p>\n<p>A <strong>magnetic lock</strong> has no mechanical way to release it. It holds only while powered, so it must be fail-safe, and the code adds extra rules to guarantee people can get out (<a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>).</p>\n<p>Which one to use depends on the door:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fire-rated doors</strong> must stay latched in a fire, so their electrified hardware is fail-secure. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80)</span></li><li><strong>Stairwell doors</strong> that are locked from the stair side often have to unlock on a fire alarm so people can get back onto a floor (<a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>).</li><li><strong>Perimeter doors</strong> are usually fail-secure so a power failure doesn't leave the building open.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: how you tell fail-safe from fail-secure on a strike that's already installed.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"1.4","title":"Power and wiring basics for access","html":"<p>If you've done intrusion work, the electrical basics are the same: Ohm's law, voltage drop, series and parallel (see the Intrusion pack's Foundations module). What's different is the <strong>load</strong>: locks draw a lot more current than motion detectors.</p>\n<ul><li>Locks run on <strong>12 VDC or 24 VDC</strong>. Many are field-selectable with a jumper or by wiring. Setting a 12 V lock on 24 V will burn out its coil; setting a 24 V lock on 12 V gives a weak hold or no release.</li><li>A magnetic lock commonly draws about <strong>0.5 A at 12 V</strong> (half that at 24 V), all the time it's locked. An electric strike draws a few hundred milliamps, only while unlocked. <span class=\"src\">(Typical spec sheets)</span></li><li>Most access power is <strong>Class 2</strong>: the supply is limited (no more than 30 V and 100 VA per output for the voltages we use) so the wiring doesn't present a fire or shock hazard. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725; Ch. 9 Table 11)</span></li><li>Class 2 wiring stays <strong>separated from 120 V wiring</strong>: not in the same raceway, box, or enclosure compartment unless a listed barrier separates them. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725.136)</span></li><li>A lock is a coil. When its power is cut, the collapsing magnetic field kicks back a voltage spike that can reset or damage the controller. A <strong>suppression diode</strong> at the lock stops it (<a href=\"#/access/learn/5\">Module 5</a>).</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"1.5","title":"Safety and the life safety mindset","html":"<p>Access control is security equipment installed on <strong>exit doors</strong>. A mistake can trap people in a fire. Treat every door as part of the building's life safety system.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Never leave a door unable to open from the egress side</strong>, even for a few minutes while you work. If you have to take hardware off, prop the door, post someone at it, or leave it unlocked, and tell the building contact. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 Ch. 7)</span></li><li><strong>Tell the building before you work.</strong> People may get locked out, doors may go unlocked, and forced and held-open alarms may report to a monitoring center. Put the account on test if door alarms are monitored. <span class=\"src\">(Industry practice)</span></li><li><strong>Don't touch the fire alarm release without the fire alarm contractor.</strong> The connection that unlocks doors on a fire alarm belongs to both systems. Testing it means putting the fire alarm account on test and following its testing rules (see the Fire pack's testing module). <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>Ladders and doors:</strong> a door swinging into your ladder is the classic access injury. Lock the door open or post someone at it while you work overhead.</li><li><strong>Lock out 120 V</strong> before working on a power supply's AC input, and disconnect the battery before you change fuses or outputs.</li><li><strong>Drilling doors and frames:</strong> find out if the door is fire-rated (label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame's hinge rabbet) before you drill anything (<a href=\"#/access/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Name the three jobs an access control system does.","Which device tells the controller whether the door is closed?","What does a fail-safe lock do when it loses power?","A fail-secure strike loses power. Can someone inside still get out? Why?","Why must a magnetic lock be fail-safe?","What happens if you power a 12 V lock with 24 V?","Why can't Class 2 lock wiring share a junction box with 120 V wiring?","Before you take a lock off an exit door, what must you do?"],"answers":["Keep unauthorized people out, let everyone out freely, and report what happened","The door position switch (DPS)","It unlocks","Yes. The lever or exit device on the egress side releases the latch mechanically, whatever the strike does","It has no mechanical release, so if it held without power people couldn't get out","The coil overheats and can burn out","The code requires Class 2 wiring to be separated from power wiring unless a listed barrier separates them","Make sure the door can still be opened from the egress side (prop it, post someone, or leave it unlocked) and tell the building contact"],"keyHtml":"1) Keep unauthorized people out, let everyone out freely, and report what happened. 2) The door position switch (DPS). 3) It unlocks. 4) Yes. The lever or exit device on the egress side releases the latch mechanically, whatever the strike does. 5) It has no mechanical release, so if it held without power people couldn't get out. 6) The coil overheats and can burn out. 7) The code requires Class 2 wiring to be separated from power wiring unless a listed barrier separates them. 8) Make sure the door can still be opened from the egress side (prop it, post someone, or leave it unlocked) and tell the building contact.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":2,"title":"How an Access System Works","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> follow a badge read from the reader to the lock and back, explain the main controller architectures, set up the building blocks of a database (people, access levels, schedules), and describe what the system reports.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"2.1","title":"One badge read, step by step","html":"<p>Everything an access system does is built on this sequence.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Present.</strong> The user holds a card or phone to the reader.</li><li><strong>Read.</strong> The reader pulls the credential number and sends it to the controller (over Wiegand or OSDP wiring, <a href=\"#/access/learn/3\">Module 3</a>).</li><li><strong>Decide.</strong> The controller looks the number up in its <strong>local database</strong>: is the card valid, is it allowed at this door, is it allowed right now?</li><li><strong>Grant.</strong> If yes, the controller energizes (or de-energizes) its <strong>lock relay</strong> for the <strong>unlock time</strong> (commonly a few seconds), turns the reader LED green, and <strong>shunts</strong> the door alarm so opening the door isn't a forced-door alarm.</li><li><strong>Open and close.</strong> The DPS shows the door open, then closed. The lock relocks when the unlock time ends or when the door closes, depending on how it's programmed.</li><li><strong>Log.</strong> The event (who, which door, when, granted or denied) goes to the software.</li></ol>\n<p>If the card is denied, the LED shows red, nothing unlocks, and the event is logged as a denial with the reason (unknown card, wrong time, wrong door, expired).</p>\n<p><strong>The controller decides, not the server.</strong> Good controllers keep their own copy of the database, so doors keep working when the network or server is down. Events are stored at the controller and uploaded when it reconnects.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.2","title":"Controller architectures","html":"<p>You'll see four common layouts in the field.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Layout</th><th>How it works</th><th>Where you see it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Standalone</strong></td><td>Keypad or reader and lock in one unit at the door, programmed at the door</td><td>Single doors, small offices, storage rooms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Panel-based (centralized)</strong></td><td>A multi-door controller in a closet, home-run cable to every door</td><td>Most commercial buildings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edge / PoE</strong></td><td>A small controller at or above each door, powered by and talking over the network cable</td><td>Newer buildings, retrofits with good network</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cloud-hosted</strong></td><td>Any of the above, with the software running in the cloud instead of on a local server</td><td>Growing quickly in small and mid-size sites</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Panel-based</strong> systems usually have a main controller that talks to the software and <strong>door interface modules</strong> (sub-controllers) that each handle one to four doors. The main controller to module link is often RS-485.</p>\n<p><strong>Home-run cabling</strong> for a panel-based door typically means separate cables for the reader, the lock, the DPS, and the REX, all landing at the controller. Composite cables carry all of them in one jacket (<a href=\"#/access/learn/5\">Module 5</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Wireless and offline locks</strong> are a separate category: battery-powered locksets that read the card at the door and talk to a hub (or get updates from the cards themselves). They save wiring but have their own battery maintenance.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.3","title":"The database: people, access levels, schedules","html":"<p>Every access system organizes permissions the same way.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Cardholders</strong> are the people. Each has one or more <strong>credentials</strong>.</li><li><strong>Doors</strong> (and readers) are the hardware.</li><li><strong>Schedules</strong> (time zones) are blocks of time: &quot;Weekdays 7 am to 6 pm.&quot;</li><li><strong>Access levels</strong> (access groups) tie doors to schedules: &quot;Main entrance and office suite, weekdays 7 to 6.&quot;</li><li>Each cardholder gets one or more access levels.</li></ul>\n<p>So you never give a person a door directly; you give them an access level, and the access level gives them doors and times. When a new door is added to a suite, you add it to the access level once and everyone who needs it gets it.</p>\n<p>Other common features:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Holidays:</strong> days when normal schedules don't apply.</li><li><strong>Door schedules (auto-unlock):</strong> the main entrance unlocks itself during business hours. Good practice is <strong>first-person-in</strong>: it stays locked until a valid card is used after the scheduled time, so a snow day doesn't leave the building open.</li><li><strong>Anti-passback:</strong> a card used to enter must be used to exit before it can enter again. Stops one card being passed back to a second person.</li><li><strong>Two-person rule, card plus PIN, elevator floors:</strong> higher-security options you'll meet on larger sites.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the schedule mistake you see most on service calls.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"2.4","title":"Events, alarms, and integrations","html":"<p>The system watches each door with the DPS and REX.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>What happened</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Access granted / denied</strong></td><td>A credential was read</td><td>Normal use</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Door forced open</strong></td><td>The door opened with no valid card and no REX</td><td>Break-in, a REX that didn't trip, a bad DPS, a worn latch that let the door pop open</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Door held open</strong></td><td>The door stayed open longer than the held-open time</td><td>Propped door, slow closer, deliveries</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tamper</strong></td><td>A reader, controller, or enclosure was opened</td><td>Vandalism, or a tech who didn't put the account on test</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Offline / comm loss</strong></td><td>The controller or a reader stopped talking</td><td>Network, cable, or power problem</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AC loss / low battery</strong></td><td>Power supply troubles, if the supply is monitored</td><td>Breaker, failing battery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Integrations</strong> you'll meet:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fire alarm:</strong> fail-safe locks on egress doors release when the fire alarm activates. This connection is a life safety function (<a href=\"#/access/learn/6\">Module 6</a> and <a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>).</li><li><strong>Intrusion:</strong> badging in can disarm an area; the access system can report door alarms to the intrusion panel.</li><li><strong>Video:</strong> door events pull up camera clips.</li><li><strong>Elevators:</strong> the reader in the cab controls which floor buttons work.</li><li><strong>Intercom and visitor management:</strong> remote unlock and visitor badges.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["List the six steps of a badge read.","Where does the decision to unlock happen in a well-designed system?","What does &quot;shunting&quot; the door alarm mean during a valid read?","What's the difference between a panel-based and an edge controller layout?","A new door is added to the accounting suite. How do you give everyone in accounting access with one change?","What does first-person-in do on an auto-unlock schedule?","A door shows &quot;forced open&quot; but nobody broke in. Name two likely causes.","Which integration is a life safety function?"],"answers":["Present, read, decide, grant, open and close, log","In the controller, using its local database","The controller ignores the door opening for that event so it isn't reported as forced","Panel-based has a central controller with cable home-run to each door; edge puts a small controller at each door, usually powered over the network","Add the door to the accounting access level","The door stays locked after the schedule starts until a valid card is used","Any two of: a REX that didn't trip, a bad or misaligned DPS, a worn latch that lets the door pop open","The fire alarm release of fail-safe locks on egress doors"],"keyHtml":"1) Present, read, decide, grant, open and close, log. 2) In the controller, using its local database. 3) The controller ignores the door opening for that event so it isn't reported as forced. 4) Panel-based has a central controller with cable home-run to each door; edge puts a small controller at each door, usually powered over the network. 5) Add the door to the accounting access level. 6) The door stays locked after the schedule starts until a valid card is used. 7) Any two of: a REX that didn't trip, a bad or misaligned DPS, a worn latch that lets the door pop open. 8) The fire alarm release of fail-safe locks on egress doors.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":3,"title":"Credentials and Readers","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> tell credential technologies apart, read a Wiegand card number, wire a Wiegand or OSDP reader, and mount a reader where everyone can reach it.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"3.1","title":"Credential technologies","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>How it works</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>125 kHz proximity (prox)</strong></td><td>Card broadcasts a fixed number when the reader's field powers it</td><td>Still everywhere. <strong>Easy to copy</strong> with cheap cloners; no encryption</td></tr><tr><td><strong>13.56 MHz smart card</strong></td><td>Card has a chip that answers the reader with encrypted data</td><td>The modern standard. Several competing families; the reader must support the card's family and keys</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mobile credential</strong></td><td>Phone app talks to the reader over Bluetooth or NFC</td><td>No cards to print; needs readers that support it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PIN</strong></td><td>User types a code on a keypad</td><td>Shareable, so usually used with a card (card plus PIN)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Biometric</strong></td><td>Fingerprint, face, or iris</td><td>Can't be lent out; enrollment and privacy rules apply</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long range</strong></td><td>UHF tags or vehicle tags read from several feet away</td><td>Parking gates and vehicle entrances</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Multi-technology readers</strong> read both prox and smart cards. They're the usual way to migrate a site: install new readers that read the old prox cards, then reissue cards over time and turn prox off.</p>\n<p><strong>Card numbers aren't secret on prox.</strong> If the customer cares about security, recommend smart cards or mobile with encryption turned on, not just a smart card reading the card's serial number.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.2","title":"Wiegand","html":"<p>Wiegand is the most common reader-to-controller wiring. It's old, simple, and one-way: the reader sends the card number and the controller sends back LED and beeper signals on separate wires.</p>\n<p><strong>Wires</strong> (the most common color convention; always check the reader's manual): <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer convention)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Color</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red</td><td>+ power (usually 12 VDC)</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td>Ground</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>Data 0 (D0)</td></tr><tr><td>White</td><td>Data 1 (D1)</td></tr><tr><td>Brown or orange</td><td>LED control</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Beeper control</td></tr><tr><td>Bare / drain</td><td>Shield</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>D0 and D1 sit at about 5 V when idle and pulse low to send a 0 (on D0) or a 1 (on D1). Swap them and the controller gets the wrong number or nothing at all.</p>\n<p><strong>The 26-bit format.</strong> The classic Wiegand number is 26 bits: <span class=\"src\">(26-bit Wiegand format)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bit 1</th><th>Bits 2 to 9</th><th>Bits 10 to 25</th><th>Bit 26</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Even parity</td><td><strong>Facility code</strong> (0 to 255)</td><td><strong>Card number</strong> (0 to 65,535)</td><td>Odd parity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Only 65,536 card numbers per facility code means duplicates between sites are common. Larger formats (34-bit, 35-bit, 37-bit, and proprietary formats) give more numbers. The controller must be set to the format the cards use.</p>\n<p><strong>Distance:</strong> Wiegand is good to about <strong>500 ft</strong> using 22 AWG shielded cable. <span class=\"src\">(SIA AC-01; manufacturer manuals)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> Wiegand is unencrypted and unsupervised. Someone with access to the wires at the reader can capture card numbers or replay them, and the controller can't tell if a reader has been swapped. That's why OSDP is replacing it.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.3","title":"OSDP","html":"<p><strong>OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol)</strong> is the modern reader standard, published by the Security Industry Association. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Two-way.</strong> The controller polls the reader; the reader answers. If a reader stops answering, the controller knows right away (it's <strong>supervised</strong>).</li><li><strong>RS-485 wiring:</strong> two data wires (often labeled A/B or +/−) plus power and ground. Four conductors, twisted pair for the data.</li><li><strong>Multi-drop:</strong> several readers can share one RS-485 run, each with its own address. Wire it as a daisy chain, not a star. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></li><li><strong>Distance:</strong> up to about <strong>4,000 ft</strong> on RS-485 with proper twisted pair cable. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP; TIA-485)</span></li><li><strong>Termination:</strong> long runs get a 120 Ω terminating resistor at each end of the bus. Short runs often don't need it; follow the controller manual. <span class=\"src\">(TIA-485; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li><strong>Secure Channel</strong> encrypts the link (AES-128). Turn it on: OSDP without Secure Channel is supervised but not encrypted. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></li><li><strong>Settings must match</strong> on both ends: address, baud rate (commonly 9600 by default), and Secure Channel keys.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Retrofits:</strong> OSDP usually runs on the existing Wiegand cable, using one twisted pair for data. Check that the old cable has a twisted pair available.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: what you check first when an OSDP reader won't come online.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"3.4","title":"Mounting and wiring the reader","html":"<p><strong>Height and reach.</strong> Card readers and keypads are operable parts, so they go within the accessible reach range: no higher than <strong>48 in</strong> and no lower than <strong>15 in</strong> above the floor, with clear floor space in front. Many specs call for 42 to 48 in to the center of the reader. <span class=\"src\">(ADA 308)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Location.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>On the <strong>latch side</strong> of the door, close enough that a user can badge and pull the door in one motion.</li><li>On the wall or mullion, not on the door, unless it's a reader built into the lockset.</li><li><strong>Metal mullions</strong> detune readers and cut read range. Use a mullion-mount reader or a spacer, and test range after mounting.</li><li>Keep readers away from each other (back-to-back readers on two sides of a wall can interfere).</li><li>Outdoors: a reader rated for the weather, with the cable entry sealed and a drip loop.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Cable.</strong> Typical Wiegand reader cable is <strong>22 AWG, 6 conductors, shielded</strong> (8 conductors if the reader needs more functions). Ground the shield drain <strong>at the controller end only</strong>; at the reader, cut it back and tape it so it can't touch anything. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Power.</strong> Most readers want 12 VDC and draw 50 to 250 mA (more when a heater or LCD is on). Long runs on 22 AWG drop voltage; check with the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/reader\">Reader Cable calculator</a></strong>.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why are 125 kHz prox cards considered insecure?","What does a multi-technology reader let you do on a migration?","On a typical Wiegand reader, what are the green and white wires?","In a 26-bit Wiegand number, what range can the facility code be?","What's the usual maximum distance for a Wiegand reader run?","Name two advantages OSDP has over Wiegand.","How should several OSDP readers on one RS-485 run be wired?","What's the highest an operable part of a card reader can be mounted?","Where is the reader cable's shield grounded?"],"answers":["They broadcast a fixed, unencrypted number that cheap cloners can copy","Read both the old prox cards and new smart cards, so cards can be replaced over time","Green is Data 0, white is Data 1","0 to 255","About 500 ft on 22 AWG shielded cable","Any two of: supervised (the controller knows if a reader drops off), encrypted with Secure Channel, two-way, multi-drop, longer distance","As a daisy chain, each reader with its own address, terminated at the ends on long runs","48 in above the floor","At the controller end only"],"keyHtml":"1) They broadcast a fixed, unencrypted number that cheap cloners can copy. 2) Read both the old prox cards and new smart cards, so cards can be replaced over time. 3) Green is Data 0, white is Data 1. 4) 0 to 255. 5) About 500 ft on 22 AWG shielded cable. 6) Any two of: supervised (the controller knows if a reader drops off), encrypted with Secure Channel, two-way, multi-drop, longer distance. 7) As a daisy chain, each reader with its own address, terminated at the ends on long runs. 8) 48 in above the floor. 9) At the controller end only.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":4,"title":"Locks and Door Hardware","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> read a door's handing and hardware, choose between strikes, magnetic locks, electrified locksets, and electrified exit devices, install a DPS and REX that report correctly, and recognize the rules for fire-rated doors.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Door anatomy and handing","html":"<p>Know the names before you order hardware.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Frame:</strong> head (top), jambs (sides), stop (the lip the door closes against). <strong>Hollow metal</strong> frames are steel; <strong>aluminum storefront</strong> frames are thin-walled tube; wood frames are common in residential and interiors.</li><li><strong>Door:</strong> hinge edge, lock edge (latch edge), push side, pull side.</li><li><strong>Hardware:</strong> hinges, lockset or exit device, closer, stop, and the <strong>door hardware schedule</strong> that lists every piece by door number on commercial jobs.</li><li><strong>Handing:</strong> stand on the <strong>outside</strong> (the secure, keyed side). Hinges on the left and the door swings away from you: left hand (LH). Hinges on the right, swinging away: right hand (RH). Swinging toward you: left hand reverse (LHR) or right hand reverse (RHR). Strikes, exit devices, and some maglock brackets are handed.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Read the existing hardware first.</strong> The lock that's already in the door decides what electric strike will work with it (latch type, backset, and whether it has a deadlatch).</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the quick way you check handing and backset at a door before ordering.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"4.2","title":"Electric strikes","html":"<p>An electric strike replaces the frame's strike plate. When it releases, its <strong>keeper</strong> swings free so the latch can pass without being retracted. The lockset stays mechanical.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fail-secure</strong> is the most common: locked without power, unlocked when energized. The door's own lever always lets people out, so egress is unaffected.</li><li><strong>Fail-safe</strong> strikes exist for doors that must unlock on power loss or fire alarm, but <strong>not on fire-rated doors</strong> (<a href=\"#/access/learn/4/4.5\">Lesson 4.5</a>).</li><li><strong>Match the lock:</strong> the strike must fit the latch type (cylindrical, mortise, rim exit device), the latch throw, and the deadlatch. A deadlatch that falls into the keeper gap leaves the door unlockable or unlatched.</li><li><strong>Preload:</strong> a door that's pushing or pulling on the latch (weatherstrip, warped door, stack pressure) can bind the keeper so it won't release. Strikes are rated for a preload; when it's exceeded, fix the door or use a high-preload strike.</li><li><strong>Current:</strong> draws only while unlocked, commonly a few hundred milliamps at 12 V. Continuous-duty strikes are needed when a door is held unlocked on a schedule. <span class=\"src\">(Typical spec sheets)</span></li><li><strong>Rim strikes</strong> go with rim exit devices (panic bars) and mount on the surface of the frame.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"4.3","title":"Magnetic locks","html":"<p>A <strong>magnetic lock (maglock)</strong> is an electromagnet on the frame header and a steel <strong>armature plate</strong> on the door. Energized, it holds the door shut; de-energized, it lets go.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Holding force</strong> is commonly 600 lb (interior) or 1,200 lb (perimeter). Current is commonly about 0.5 A at 12 V or 0.25 A at 24 V, drawn <strong>all the time the door is locked</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(Typical spec sheets)</span></li><li><strong>Always fail-safe.</strong> Because it holds with no mechanical release, egress must be guaranteed electrically: the code requires either a motion sensor plus push button arrangement or a switch built into the door hardware (<a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>).</li><li>The magnet always mounts on the <strong>secure side</strong>. On a door that swings away from the secure side (the usual out-swinging exit door), it mounts under the header on the push side, with an L bracket if the frame is narrow. On a door that swings toward the secure side, a Z bracket brings the magnet down to meet the armature on the pull side.</li><li><strong>Armature</strong> must float on its rubber washers so it can align itself flat with the magnet. A tightened-down armature won't seat flat and holds poorly.</li><li><strong>Residual magnetism:</strong> a worn or dirty magnet can hold slightly after release. Most maglocks have a built-in suppression and anti-residual circuit; keep faces clean.</li><li><strong>Bond sensor</strong> and <strong>DPS</strong> options report whether the magnet is actually holding.</li></ul>\n<p>Maglocks are easy to install on doors where nothing else fits (glass doors, existing panic hardware), which is why they're overused. Many AHJs restrict them; check before you quote one.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.4","title":"Electrified locksets, exit devices, and power transfer","html":"<ul><li><strong>Electrified lockset</strong> (cylindrical or mortise): the lock itself has a solenoid or motor that locks or unlocks the outside lever. The inside lever is always free. Fail-secure or fail-safe by order or by setting. Usually the cleanest, most secure option.</li><li><strong>Electrified exit device (panic bar):</strong> either <strong>electric latch retraction</strong> (pulls the latch in so the door is push/pull, often used with door operators) or <strong>electrified trim</strong> (unlocks the outside lever).</li><li><strong>Solenoid latch retraction</strong> can pull a large <strong>inrush current</strong> for a fraction of a second (several amps). It needs a power supply or controller made for it. Motorized latch retraction draws much less. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></li><li><strong>Request-to-exit switch</strong> built into the lockset or exit device: reports that the inside hardware was used.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Getting power into the door:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Electrified hinge</strong> (power transfer hinge): wires run through the hinge. Clean and protected.</li><li><strong>Concealed power transfer:</strong> a device mortised into the door edge and frame.</li><li><strong>Door loop:</strong> an armored flexible loop between frame and door. Cheapest, most exposed.</li></ul>\n<p>All three limit wire count and wire size. Check the conductor gauge in the hinge against the lock's current and the run length (voltage drop, <a href=\"#/access/learn/5\">Module 5</a>).</p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.5","title":"DPS, REX, and fire-rated doors","html":"<p><strong>Door position switch (DPS).</strong> A recessed magnetic contact in the frame head near the latch side (or a surface contact). Most controllers want it closed when the door is closed. Mount it where it will show &quot;open&quot; before the door has moved far enough to let someone through.</p>\n<p><strong>REX.</strong> Tells the controller someone's leaving so the door opening isn't reported as forced.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Motion REX:</strong> a PIR mounted above the door on the egress side, aimed down at the area in front of the door. Aim it so it <strong>can't be tripped from the secure side</strong> through the gap under or between doors (people slide things under or use air or smoke through the gap).</li><li><strong>Hardware REX:</strong> a switch in the lever or exit device. More reliable for reporting.</li><li>On a fail-secure strike, the REX usually <strong>only shunts</strong> the alarm; the lever releases the latch mechanically.</li><li>On a maglock, the REX may also be one of the ways the lock is released, under the code rules in <a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Fire-rated doors.</strong> Look for the label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame. A fire door must <strong>positively latch</strong> every time it closes, so:</p>\n<ul><li>Electrified hardware on it must be <strong>listed for use on fire doors</strong>, and electric strikes on fire doors must be <strong>fail-secure</strong>. A fail-safe strike would leave the door unlatched in a fire. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80)</span></li><li><strong>Don't field-modify</strong> a fire door or frame (cutting, drilling, enlarging preps) beyond what the fire door standard allows. Most modifications need factory or listed-agency preparation; otherwise the label is void. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80 Ch. 4)</span></li><li>Don't add a hold-open or remove the closer to make a door easier to use. Fire doors close and latch.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Standing on the outside, hinges on the right, door swings away from you. What hand is the door?","Why does an electric strike need to match the lockset that's already there?","What is strike preload, and what does it cause?","Typical maglock current at 12 V, and when does it draw it?","Why should a maglock's armature be left free to float?","What's special about solenoid latch retraction exit devices for power?","Name the three ways to get power into a door.","Where should a motion REX be aimed, and what must it not see?","Can you put a fail-safe electric strike on a fire-rated door? Why?"],"answers":["Right hand (RH)","The keeper must fit the latch type, throw, and deadlatch or the door won't lock or latch correctly","The door pushing or pulling on the latch; it binds the keeper so it won't release","About 0.5 A, all the time the door is locked","So it can align flat with the magnet; a tight armature holds poorly","They pull a large inrush current for an instant and need a power supply made for it","Electrified hinge, concealed power transfer, or a door loop","Down at the area in front of the door on the egress side; it must not be trippable from the secure side through door gaps","No. Fire doors must positively latch, so electric strikes on them must be fail-secure"],"keyHtml":"1) Right hand (RH). 2) The keeper must fit the latch type, throw, and deadlatch or the door won't lock or latch correctly. 3) The door pushing or pulling on the latch; it binds the keeper so it won't release. 4) About 0.5 A, all the time the door is locked. 5) So it can align flat with the magnet; a tight armature holds poorly. 6) They pull a large inrush current for an instant and need a power supply made for it. 7) Electrified hinge, concealed power transfer, or a door loop. 8) Down at the area in front of the door on the egress side; it must not be trippable from the secure side through door gaps. 9) No. Fire doors must positively latch, so electric strikes on them must be fail-secure.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":5,"title":"Power and Wiring","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> size an access power supply and its batteries, keep lock power from upsetting the controller, pick the right cable for each device, and check voltage drop to the lock and reader.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"5.1","title":"Access power supplies","html":"<p>Access power supplies are built for locks: a Class 2 supply with a battery charger and several <strong>individually fused or PTC-protected outputs</strong>, often with a <strong>fire alarm interface</strong> input that cuts lock power on alarm.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Listed for the job.</strong> Use a power supply listed for access control (UL 294) or burglar alarm (UL 603) use, and for fire alarm use where it's part of a fire alarm system. <span class=\"src\">(UL 294; UL 603)</span></li><li><strong>Don't load it to 100%.</strong> Design for no more than about <strong>80% of the rated output</strong>, leaving room for inrush, aging batteries, and the door someone adds next year. <span class=\"src\">(Design practice)</span></li><li><strong>Separate lock power from controller power</strong> where you can: a separate supply, or at least separate outputs. A lock's inrush and kickback on the same output as the controller is the classic cause of controllers rebooting when a door unlocks.</li><li><strong>12 or 24 V:</strong> pick one per supply and match every lock to it. 24 V halves the current for the same lock, which cuts voltage drop on long runs.</li><li><strong>Supervision:</strong> wire the supply's AC fail and low battery outputs to controller inputs so power troubles get reported.</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/lockpsu\">Access Power calculator</a></strong> to add up the load and pick a supply and battery.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.2","title":"Batteries and standby time","html":"<p>Access batteries keep doors locked (and readers working) through a power failure. How long is a design decision, not a single code number.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>UL 294 standby power levels:</strong> Level I has no standby requirement; Level II is 30 minutes; Level III is 2 hours; Level IV is 4 hours. The specification or the customer picks the level. <span class=\"src\">(UL 294)</span></li><li><strong>Formula (same as intrusion):</strong> Required Ah = load current in A × standby hours × 1.2. The 1.2 is a 20% margin for battery aging and temperature. <span class=\"src\">(Industry practice)</span></li><li><strong>Fail-safe locks drain batteries fast.</strong> Every maglock draws current the whole time it's locked. Some designs leave maglocks off the battery on purpose (they unlock in a power failure); that's a security and egress decision for the customer and AHJ, so get it in writing.</li><li><strong>Fail-secure strikes barely touch the battery.</strong> They draw only while unlocked.</li><li><strong>Two 12 V batteries in series</strong> for a 24 V supply: same size, same age.</li><li>Sealed lead-acid batteries are commonly replaced every <strong>3 to 5 years</strong>, and dated when installed. <span class=\"src\">(Battery manufacturers)</span></li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"5.3","title":"Suppression and kickback","html":"<p>When power to a lock coil is cut, the collapsing magnetic field produces a voltage spike in the opposite direction, sometimes hundreds of volts for an instant. It arcs relay contacts, resets controllers, and garbles reader data.</p>\n<ul><li>On <strong>DC locks</strong>, put a <strong>diode</strong> (commonly a 1N4001 to 1N4007) across the lock terminals, <strong>at the lock</strong>, reverse biased: the band (cathode) to +, the other end to −. Installed backward, it's a short circuit across the lock output. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></li><li>Many maglocks and some strikes have <strong>built-in suppression</strong>. Check before adding a diode.</li><li><strong>AC strikes</strong> use a <strong>MOV</strong> (metal oxide varistor) instead, since a diode would block AC.</li><li>At the lock is the right place. A diode back at the controller still lets the spike travel the whole cable run next to reader wiring.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the symptom that made you start putting a diode on every lock.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"5.4","title":"Cable","html":"<p><strong>A typical home-run door, panel-based controller</strong> (check the manufacturer's wiring diagram):</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Common cable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reader (Wiegand)</td><td>22 AWG, 6 conductor, shielded (8 conductor for extra functions)</td></tr><tr><td>Reader (OSDP)</td><td>22 AWG, 2 twisted pair (data pair plus power pair), shielded</td></tr><tr><td>Lock</td><td>18 AWG, 2 conductor (16 AWG or heavier on long runs or high current)</td></tr><tr><td>DPS</td><td>22 AWG, 2 conductor</td></tr><tr><td>REX (motion)</td><td>22 AWG, 4 conductor (power plus relay contact)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Composite cable</strong> puts all of these in one jacket and is common on new installs.</p>\n<p><strong>Cable ratings</strong> (NEC power-limited cable types): <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725 / 722)</span></p>\n<ul><li><strong>CL2 / CL3:</strong> general use.</li><li><strong>CL2R / CL3R:</strong> riser, between floors in a shaft.</li><li><strong>CL2P / CL3P:</strong> plenum, in air-handling spaces such as return air ceilings.</li><li>Communications cable ratings (CM, CMR, CMP) of the same or higher level are allowed substitutes.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Support:</strong> cable is supported by the building structure with listed hardware, not laid on ceiling tiles or tied to ceiling grid wires, pipes, or conduit. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725.24; 300.11)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Network cable:</strong> Ethernet (Category 5e/6) to IP controllers and PoE devices is limited to <strong>100 m (328 ft)</strong> per run. <span class=\"src\">(TIA-568)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.5","title":"Voltage drop to the door","html":"<p>Locks are the heaviest load you'll put on long, thin wire, and a lock that gets too little voltage holds weakly or won't release.</p>\n<p><strong>Voltage drop = 2 × one-way length × current × Ω per foot</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> A maglock draws 0.5 A at 12 V, 200 ft from the power supply on 18 AWG (0.00639 Ω/ft). Drop = 2 × 200 × 0.5 × 0.00639 = <strong>1.28 V</strong>. The lock sees about 10.7 V.</p>\n<p>Most locks want to see within about 10% of their rated voltage (check the spec sheet); 10.7 V is borderline. The fixes: 16 AWG, set the lock and supply to 24 V (half the current, a quarter of the voltage loss as a percentage), or put a power supply closer to the door.</p>\n<ul><li>Measure lock voltage <strong>at the lock, with the lock energized</strong>. An unloaded reading tells you nothing.</li><li>Count the electrified hinge or power transfer: its thin conductors are part of the run.</li><li><strong>PoE edge devices:</strong> budget the power. A standard PoE port supplies about 15.4 W (about 12.95 W reaches the device); PoE+ supplies 30 W (25.5 W at the device). Locks powered from a PoE controller must fit inside what's left after the controller and reader. <span class=\"src\">(IEEE 802.3af/at)</span></li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/drop\">Voltage Drop calculator</a></strong> for lock runs and the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/reader\">Reader Cable calculator</a></strong> for readers.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why design a power supply to about 80% of its rating?","Why separate lock power from controller power?","What are the UL 294 standby levels?","A site needs 2 hours of standby for 1.5 A of locks and readers. How many amp-hours, including the margin?","Which way does a suppression diode go across a DC lock, and where?","What do you use instead of a diode on an AC strike?","What cable rating do you need above a return-air ceiling?","A 12 V maglock draws 0.5 A, 150 ft away on 18 AWG. What's the drop?","How should you measure the voltage at a lock?"],"answers":["To leave room for inrush, battery aging, and added doors","Lock inrush and kickback on the controller's supply can reboot it or garble reader data","Level I none, Level II 30 minutes, Level III 2 hours, Level IV 4 hours","1.5 × 2 × 1.2 = 3.6 Ah","Reverse biased (band to +), at the lock","A MOV","Plenum: CL2P (or CMP)","2 × 150 × 0.5 × 0.00639 = about 0.96 V","At the lock terminals, with the lock energized"],"keyHtml":"1) To leave room for inrush, battery aging, and added doors. 2) Lock inrush and kickback on the controller's supply can reboot it or garble reader data. 3) Level I none, Level II 30 minutes, Level III 2 hours, Level IV 4 hours. 4) 1.5 × 2 × 1.2 = 3.6 Ah. 5) Reverse biased (band to +), at the lock. 6) A MOV. 7) Plenum: CL2P (or CMP). 8) 2 × 150 × 0.5 × 0.00639 = about 0.96 V. 9) At the lock terminals, with the lock energized.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":6,"title":"Installation","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> survey a door before quoting it, mount and wire each device at the door, place the controller and power supply, wire the fire alarm release correctly, and leave a door that the next tech can service.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"6.1","title":"Door survey","html":"<p>Most access problems are built in at the survey. Go to every door and write down:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Door and frame:</strong> material (hollow metal, aluminum storefront, wood, glass), handing, swing direction, condition. Does it close and latch on its own every time? If not, fix that first; an access system can't secure a door that doesn't latch.</li><li><strong>Fire label:</strong> on the door edge and in the frame. A fire door limits what hardware you can use and how you install it (<a href=\"#/access/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li><li><strong>Existing hardware:</strong> lockset type and brand, exit device, closer, hinges. Take photos of the latch, strike, and both sides of the door.</li><li><strong>Egress:</strong> is this door part of an exit path? Is panic hardware required? Will the AHJ allow a maglock here?</li><li><strong>Cable path:</strong> from the door to the controller location. Ceiling type (plenum?), walls (block, drywall), fire-rated walls you'll penetrate.</li><li><strong>Power:</strong> where the 120 V for the power supply comes from and who provides it.</li><li><strong>Network:</strong> for IP controllers, a port and an address from the customer's IT.</li><li><strong>Fire alarm:</strong> does the building have one? Where's the panel, and who services it?</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the survey question that saves you a return trip.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"6.2","title":"At the door","html":"<ul><li><strong>Reader:</strong> latch side, within reach range (<a href=\"#/access/learn/3/3.4\">Lesson 3.4</a>), on a single-gang box or surface mount. Seal exterior penetrations.</li><li><strong>Lock:</strong> follow the hardware manufacturer's template exactly. Measure twice on aluminum storefront; you can't undo a hole in a thin-wall frame.</li><li><strong>DPS:</strong> in the frame head near the latch side, magnet in the door top. Check the gap at the hinge side isn't so big that a door opened a few inches still reads closed.</li><li><strong>REX:</strong> above the door on the egress side, aimed down at the door hardware area. Walk test it from both sides of the door: it must trip from the inside and must not trip from the outside.</li><li><strong>Diode</strong> at the lock if the lock doesn't have built-in suppression (<a href=\"#/access/learn/5/5.3\">Lesson 5.3</a>).</li><li><strong>Service loop</strong> of a foot or two above the door, so the next tech can remake terminations.</li><li><strong>Fire-rated walls:</strong> seal every penetration with a listed firestop system for that wall type. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 7; NEC 300.21)</span></li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"6.3","title":"Controller and power supply","html":"<ul><li>In a <strong>secure location</strong>: a locked room, inside the secure perimeter, never on the unsecure side of the door it controls.</li><li><strong>Close to the doors</strong> it serves to keep cable runs short, but accessible for service (not over a drop ceiling above a desk if you can avoid it).</li><li><strong>Enclosure tamper</strong> switches wired and reporting.</li><li><strong>Dedicated 120 V circuit</strong> where possible, not a shared receptacle someone can unplug. Label the breaker. Make the AC connection inside the enclosure's high-voltage compartment, separated from Class 2 wiring.</li><li><strong>Grounding:</strong> earth ground the enclosure and the controller's ground terminal per the manual. One ground point per shield.</li><li><strong>Network:</strong> a static address or a DHCP reservation, documented. Ask IT whether the controller is on its own VLAN.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"6.4","title":"Fire alarm release","html":"<p>Where a building has a fire alarm system, <strong>fail-safe locks on egress doors unlock when the fire alarm activates</strong>, and stay unlocked until the fire alarm is reset. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></p>\n<p>How it's wired in practice:</p>\n<ul><li>A fire alarm <strong>relay</strong> (a control module or panel relay) opens on alarm.</li><li>That contact goes in the <strong>lock power path</strong>, typically the access power supply's <strong>fire alarm interface</strong> input, so the locks lose power directly. Don't rely on a controller input and software to unlock the doors. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>The fire alarm relay or control module goes within <strong>3 ft</strong> of the device it controls (here, the power supply's fire alarm input), with the fire alarm wiring to the relay supervised, the same rule as any fire alarm control function (see the Fire pack's lesson on control functions). <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li><strong>Fail-secure locks don't unlock on fire alarm</strong> unless they're required to (stairwell re-entry, <a href=\"#/access/learn/8\">Module 8</a>). Their egress side is already free.</li><li><strong>Who wires it:</strong> the fire alarm contractor owns the relay and its programming. Coordinate, and test the release together.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Test it</strong> at acceptance and after any change to either system: put the fire alarm account on test, activate an alarm, confirm every fail-safe door releases, reset, and confirm they relock. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"6.5","title":"Labeling and documentation","html":"<ul><li>Label <strong>both ends</strong> of every cable with the door number and device (D104-RDR, D104-LOCK).</li><li>Label power supply outputs with the doors they feed, and the fuse or PTC size.</li><li>Label the breaker for the power supply, and record it inside the enclosure.</li><li>Leave a <strong>door schedule</strong> in the enclosure or with the customer: door number, controller and port, lock type, fail-safe or fail-secure, voltage, power supply output.</li><li>Keep the wiring diagram and the controller's IP address and login with the customer's records (not taped to the enclosure).</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why check that a door closes and latches on its own before quoting access?","Where do you find a fire door's label?","How do you check a motion REX after mounting it?","Where should the controller be located relative to the door it controls?","What must you do to every penetration through a fire-rated wall?","On a fire alarm, which locks must release, and until when?","Why put the fire alarm contact in the lock power path instead of a controller input?","How close must the fire alarm relay be to the device it controls?","What goes on the label at each end of a cable?"],"answers":["The access system can't secure a door that doesn't latch; fix the door first","On the hinge edge of the door and in the frame","Walk test it: it must trip from the inside and must not trip from the outside","In a secure location, on the secure side, never on the unsecure side of that door","Seal it with a listed firestop system for that wall","Fail-safe locks on egress doors; until the fire alarm is reset","So the locks lose power directly without depending on controller electronics or software","Within 3 ft","The door number and the device"],"keyHtml":"1) The access system can't secure a door that doesn't latch; fix the door first. 2) On the hinge edge of the door and in the frame. 3) Walk test it: it must trip from the inside and must not trip from the outside. 4) In a secure location, on the secure side, never on the unsecure side of that door. 5) Seal it with a listed firestop system for that wall. 6) Fail-safe locks on egress doors; until the fire alarm is reset. 7) So the locks lose power directly without depending on controller electronics or software. 8) Within 3 ft. 9) The door number and the device.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":7,"title":"Programming and Commissioning","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> set up a door in the software, choose sensible door timers, build schedules and access levels, test every door before handoff, and train the customer.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Bringing hardware online","html":"<p>Every system differs, but the order is the same.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Controllers:</strong> give each one its network address (or bus address on RS-485), add it in the software, and confirm it shows online. Update firmware to the version the software expects before you go further.</li><li><strong>Readers:</strong> set the card format (Wiegand) or the OSDP address, baud rate, and Secure Channel. Confirm a test card reads and shows up in the event log, even as &quot;unknown card.&quot;</li><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> assign the DPS and REX inputs to each door, and set each as normally open or normally closed (and supervised with resistors if the controller supports it).</li><li><strong>Outputs:</strong> assign the lock relay, and set it for fail-safe or fail-secure so &quot;unlock&quot; does the right thing.</li><li><strong>Power supply supervision:</strong> AC fail, low battery, and tamper inputs, named so an operator knows which enclosure has the trouble.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Name everything</strong> the way the building does: &quot;D104 Main Lobby Entry,&quot; not &quot;Door 1.&quot; Operators will read these names in alarms at 2 am.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.2","title":"Door timers and modes","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th><th>Common starting point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Unlock (strike) time</strong></td><td>How long the lock stays unlocked after a valid card</td><td>3 to 5 s</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extended unlock</strong></td><td>Longer unlock for cardholders who need it (ADA)</td><td>10 to 30 s</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Held-open time</strong></td><td>How long the door can stay open before a held-open alarm</td><td>30 to 60 s; longer at loading docks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pre-alarm</strong></td><td>Reader beeps before held-open alarm</td><td>A few seconds before held-open</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relock on close</strong></td><td>Lock relocks as soon as the DPS shows closed, rather than waiting out the unlock time</td><td>On, for most doors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>REX unlocks</strong></td><td>Whether REX also unlocks the lock</td><td>Usually off for fail-secure doors; on for maglock doors per the egress design</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are customer choices, not code values; set them with the customer and write them down.</p>\n<p><strong>Door modes:</strong> card only, card plus PIN, unlocked (scheduled), locked (lockdown), first-person-in. Make sure the customer knows how to put a door back to its normal mode after an override.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.3","title":"Schedules, levels, and cardholders","html":"<ul><li>Build <strong>schedules</strong> first, then <strong>access levels</strong>, then add <strong>cardholders</strong> (<a href=\"#/access/learn/2/2.3\">Lesson 2.3</a>).</li><li><strong>Holidays</strong> go in before the first one arrives.</li><li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> give each person only the doors and times they need. &quot;All doors, all times&quot; is for a few administrators.</li><li><strong>Card enrollment:</strong> read the card at an enrollment reader or type the number printed on it. Confirm the facility code matches the system's format.</li><li><strong>Operators:</strong> each person who uses the software gets their own login with only the permissions they need. No shared &quot;admin&quot; password.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: how you handle a customer who wants everyone on &quot;all doors, all times.&quot;</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"7.4","title":"Testing every door","html":"<p>Test each door from both sides before you call it done. Write the results on a door test sheet.</p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\"><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Valid card: green LED, lock releases, door opens, relocks on close, event logged with the right name and door</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Invalid card (and a valid card outside its schedule): red LED, stays locked, denial logged</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>REX: trips from the inside, does <strong>not</strong> trip from the outside, opening the door with REX is not a forced alarm</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Forced door: open the door without a card or REX (key override or a helper inside with the REX masked), forced alarm reported</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Held open: prop it past the held-open time, alarm reported, restores when closed</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Egress: inside hardware opens the door with one motion with the lock in every state, including with power off</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Power loss: remove AC, door behaves as designed on battery; remove battery and AC, fail-safe unlocks and fail-secure stays locked from outside</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Fire alarm release (fail-safe egress doors): with the fire alarm account on test, activate an alarm, door releases; reset, door relocks <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Lock voltage at the lock, energized, within the lock's rating</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Tamper on readers and enclosures reports</span></label></li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"7.5","title":"Handoff and customer training","html":"<ul><li><strong>Train the administrator:</strong> adding and removing cardholders, lost cards, changing schedules, holiday setup, running reports, and responding to door alarms.</li><li><strong>Lost or terminated cards</strong> get <strong>disabled right away</strong>, not deleted, so their history stays in reports.</li><li><strong>Hand over:</strong> door schedule, wiring diagrams, controller addresses and admin credentials (in a sealed envelope or password manager, not an email), and the door test sheet.</li><li><strong>Explain the egress features</strong> to the building: what happens in a fire alarm and a power failure, and that doors must never be chained, padlocked, or blocked.</li><li><strong>Inspection:</strong> fire door assemblies and certain egress doors need an inspection every year with written records. Tell the customer who does it. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80 Ch. 5; NFPA 101 7.2.1.15)</span></li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why update controller firmware before you program doors?","What's a good starting unlock time, and when do you use extended unlock?","What does relock on close do?","Are door timers code values? Who decides them?","Put these in the order you build them: cardholders, schedules, access levels.","Why disable a lost card instead of deleting it?","How do you test a REX from both sides?","What should a fail-safe and a fail-secure door do with AC and battery both removed?","How often do fire door assemblies need to be inspected?"],"answers":["So the controller runs the version the software expects and you don't have to redo programming","3 to 5 seconds; extended unlock for cardholders who need more time, such as people with disabilities","Relocks as soon as the door closes instead of waiting out the unlock time","No; the customer decides them with you, and you write them down","Schedules, access levels, cardholders","The history stays in reports","It must trip from inside and must not trip from outside","Fail-safe unlocks; fail-secure stays locked from outside, and both still open from inside","Every year, with written records"],"keyHtml":"1) So the controller runs the version the software expects and you don't have to redo programming. 2) 3 to 5 seconds; extended unlock for cardholders who need more time, such as people with disabilities. 3) Relocks as soon as the door closes instead of waiting out the unlock time. 4) No; the customer decides them with you, and you write them down. 5) Schedules, access levels, cardholders. 6) The history stays in reports. 7) It must trip from inside and must not trip from outside. 8) Fail-safe unlocks; fail-secure stays locked from outside, and both still open from inside. 9) Every year, with written records.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":8,"title":"Codes, Egress, and Standards","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> name the codes and standards that govern access-controlled doors, apply the free egress rules, and recognize the requirements for sensor release, electromagnetically locked, and delayed egress doors.</p>\n<p>The building code (most of the US uses the <strong>International Building Code, IBC</strong>) and the life safety code (<strong>NFPA 101</strong>) both regulate locked exit doors, and the jurisdiction decides which edition applies. Section numbers move between editions, so this module names the topics; look up the section in the edition your AHJ has adopted.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Who's in charge and which documents apply","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it covers for access control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>IBC</strong> (building code), Chapter 10</td><td>Egress doors, locks, electrically locked doors, panic hardware, stairway doors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 101</strong> (Life Safety Code)</td><td>The same topics for buildings that enforce NFPA 101 (often healthcare, schools, and state fire marshal rules)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 80</strong></td><td>Fire doors: what hardware can go on them and how they're inspected</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 72</strong></td><td>Fire alarm, including the relays that release doors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 70 (NEC)</strong></td><td>Class 2 wiring, cable ratings, power supplies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ADA Standards</strong></td><td>Reach range, hardware height, and operating force for accessible doors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 294</strong></td><td>Listing standard for access control system units (controllers, readers, locks, power supplies) <span class=\"src\">(UL 294)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 1034, BHMA A156 series</strong></td><td>Burglary-resistant electric locks; performance grades for strikes, maglocks, and exit devices <span class=\"src\">(UL 1034; BHMA A156)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The AHJ</strong> (building official and fire marshal) has the final word. Many require a permit and a plan review for any electrically locked egress door, especially maglocks and delayed egress. Get approval <strong>before</strong> you install.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.2","title":"Free egress, one operation, and hardware","html":"<p>The rules every exit door follows, locked or not:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Free egress:</strong> an exit door opens from the egress side <strong>without a key, a tool, special knowledge, or special effort</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li><strong>One operation:</strong> releasing the door takes <strong>one</strong> motion. A lever plus a separate deadbolt or a thumb-turn is two operations, which isn't allowed on most exit doors. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li><strong>Hardware height:</strong> door handles, pulls, latches, locks, and other operating parts on accessible doors are <strong>34 to 48 in</strong> above the floor. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; ADA 404.2.7)</span></li><li><strong>Operable with one hand</strong>, without tight grasping, pinching, or twisting of the wrist, and no more than <strong>5 lbf</strong> to operate. <span class=\"src\">(ADA 309.4)</span></li><li><strong>Opening force</strong> for interior hinged doors is no more than <strong>5 lbf</strong> (fire doors excepted: the minimum the AHJ allows). <span class=\"src\">(ADA 404.2.9)</span></li><li><strong>Panic hardware</strong> is required on exit doors serving assembly and educational occupancies with <strong>50 or more</strong> people, high-hazard occupancies, and certain electrical rooms. Its actuating portion extends at least <strong>half the door width</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10)</span></li></ul>\n<p>An access system almost never changes egress on a door with a mechanical lever or panic bar: the inside hardware always opens the door. The rules in the next lessons apply when <strong>the lock itself has no mechanical release</strong> (maglocks, fail-safe locksets with no free lever) or when egress is deliberately delayed.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the egress violation you find most often on existing access doors.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"8.3","title":"Sensor release (maglock with motion and push button)","html":"<p>The most common maglock arrangement: a motion sensor on the egress side unlocks the door as someone approaches, and a push button backs it up. The code calls these <strong>sensor release of electrically locked egress doors</strong> (older editions: access-controlled egress doors). Requirements: <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.2)</span></p>\n<ol><li>A <strong>sensor on the egress side</strong> detects someone approaching and unlocks the door. Loss of power to the sensor also unlocks it.</li><li><strong>Loss of power to the lock</strong> unlocks the door.</li><li>A <strong>manual unlock button</strong> labeled <strong>&quot;PUSH TO EXIT&quot;</strong>, mounted <strong>40 to 48 in</strong> above the floor and <strong>within 5 ft</strong> of the door. Pressing it <strong>cuts power to the lock directly</strong>, independent of the access control electronics, and the door stays unlocked for <strong>at least 30 seconds</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.2)</span></li><li><strong>Fire alarm or sprinkler activation</strong> (where the building has them) unlocks the doors, and they stay unlocked until the fire alarm is reset. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>The locking system is <strong>listed to UL 294</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(UL 294)</span></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Why the push button wiring matters:</strong> the button's contact goes in series with the lock's power, usually a timed relay or a pneumatic-delay button, not into a controller input. If the controller locks up, the button still works.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.4","title":"Electromagnetically locked egress doors","html":"<p>The other code path for a maglock: the <strong>door hardware itself</strong> releases the magnet. Requirements: <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6)</span></p>\n<ol><li>The hardware on the door has an <strong>obvious method of operation</strong> under all lighting conditions and works <strong>with one hand</strong>.</li><li>Operating the hardware <strong>directly interrupts power</strong> to the maglock and <strong>unlocks the door immediately</strong> (a switch built into the lever or exit device, wired in series with the lock power).</li><li><strong>Loss of power</strong> unlocks the door.</li><li>Where <strong>panic hardware</strong> is required, operating the panic hardware releases the maglock.</li><li>The locking system is <strong>listed to UL 294</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>No motion sensor or push button is required on this path because the hardware the occupant naturally uses does the release.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.5","title":"Delayed egress and stairway doors","html":"<p><strong>Delayed egress locks</strong> hold an exit door locked for a short delay when someone pushes on it, sounding a local alarm. They're used to stop theft and patient or resident wandering. Only allowed in certain occupancies, and only where the building has a <strong>complete sprinkler system or fire detection system</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Applying a force of no more than <strong>15 lbf</strong> for no more than <strong>3 s</strong> starts an <strong>irreversible</strong> release; the door <strong>unlocks within 15 s</strong> (up to 30 s if the AHJ approves). A local audible signal sounds when the process starts. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>Once released, it <strong>rearms only manually</strong> at the door. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>It <strong>releases immediately</strong> on sprinkler or fire detection activation, on loss of power, and from the fire command center where one exists. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>A sign on the door, above and within <strong>12 in</strong> of the release hardware: <strong>&quot;PUSH UNTIL ALARM SOUNDS. DOOR CAN BE OPENED IN 15 SECONDS.&quot;</strong> (30 where that's approved), in letters at least <strong>1 in</strong> high with <strong>1/8 in</strong> stroke. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>An occupant generally can't be made to pass through <strong>more than one</strong> delayed egress door before reaching an exit. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Stairway doors.</strong> In multistory buildings, doors from an exit stair back into a floor generally must allow <strong>re-entry</strong>. Where they're locked from the stair side, they must unlock automatically on a fire alarm and from the fire command center where there is one (the exact rules depend on building height and the code edition). This is usually fail-safe hardware on the stair side. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.6","title":"NEC, ADA, and inspection","html":"<ul><li><strong>NEC:</strong> Class 2 power (<a href=\"#/access/learn/1/1.4\">Lesson 1.4</a>), separation from power wiring, listed cable for the space (CL2, CL2R, CL2P), and support from the structure (<a href=\"#/access/learn/5\">Module 5</a>). <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725; Ch. 9 Table 11)</span></li><li><strong>ADA:</strong> readers within the <strong>15 to 48 in</strong> reach range; hardware 34 to 48 in; operable with one hand. <span class=\"src\">(ADA 308)</span></li><li><strong>Fire doors (NFPA 80):</strong> listed hardware, positive latching, no unapproved field modifications (<a href=\"#/access/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li><li><strong>Annual inspection:</strong> fire door assemblies get an inspection and test <strong>every year</strong> by a qualified person, with a written record. NFPA 101 also requires annual inspection of certain egress doors, including doors with panic hardware and electrically controlled egress. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80 Ch. 5; NFPA 101 7.2.1.15)</span></li><li><strong>Documentation:</strong> keep the permit, the approved drawings, door test sheets, and the fire alarm release test with the customer's records.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why does this module name topics instead of exact code section numbers?","What does &quot;free egress&quot; mean?","A door has a lever and a separate deadbolt. What rule does it break?","What height range applies to door hardware on accessible doors?","On a sensor release maglock door, what does the PUSH TO EXIT button have to do?","How is an electromagnetically locked egress door released, and what extra devices does it need?","What force and time start the release on a delayed egress door, and how long until it unlocks?","What three events release a delayed egress lock immediately?","What does a locked stairway door need to do on a fire alarm?","How often are fire door assemblies inspected?"],"answers":["Section numbers change between code editions; the AHJ's adopted edition decides","The door opens from the egress side without a key, tool, special knowledge, or special effort","One operation: releasing the door must take one motion","34 to 48 in above the floor","Be within 5 ft and 40 to 48 in high, cut lock power directly, and keep the door unlocked at least 30 seconds","A switch in the door hardware cuts lock power directly; no motion sensor or push button is needed","No more than 15 lbf for no more than 3 s; unlocks within 15 s (30 s if approved)","Sprinkler or fire detection activation, loss of power, and a signal from the fire command center","Unlock automatically to allow re-entry","Every year"],"keyHtml":"1) Section numbers change between code editions; the AHJ's adopted edition decides. 2) The door opens from the egress side without a key, tool, special knowledge, or special effort. 3) One operation: releasing the door must take one motion. 4) 34 to 48 in above the floor. 5) Be within 5 ft and 40 to 48 in high, cut lock power directly, and keep the door unlocked at least 30 seconds. 6) A switch in the door hardware cuts lock power directly; no motion sensor or push button is needed. 7) No more than 15 lbf for no more than 3 s; unlocks within 15 s (30 s if approved). 8) Sprinkler or fire detection activation, loss of power, and a signal from the fire command center. 9) Unlock automatically to allow re-entry. 10) Every year.","noteHtml":""}}]},"reference":{"introHtml":"<p>Quick cards for the Reference tab. Each <code>##</code> section is one card. Values are common US figures; the approved drawings, the AHJ's adopted code editions, and the manufacturer's instructions always win.</p>","cards":[{"id":"access-door-at-a-glance","title":"Access door at a glance","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>        SECURE SIDE              |          EGRESS SIDE\n                                 |\n  [READER]   [DPS in frame head] |  [REX motion above door]\n     |              |  DOOR      |\n     |              | [LOCK]     |  [lever / exit device]\n     |              |            |\n  [CONTROLLER] -- [POWER SUPPLY] -- fire alarm release input</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Cable (typical)</th><th>Controller connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reader</td><td>22/6 shielded (Wiegand) or 22/2 pair + power (OSDP)</td><td>Reader port</td></tr><tr><td>Lock</td><td>18/2 (heavier on long runs)</td><td>Lock relay through the power supply</td></tr><tr><td>DPS</td><td>22/2</td><td>Input, closed when door is closed</td></tr><tr><td>REX</td><td>22/4</td><td>Input; may also cut lock power</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"fail-safe-vs-fail-secure","title":"Fail-safe vs fail-secure","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Power off</th><th>Power on</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fail-safe</strong></td><td>Unlocked</td><td>Locked</td><td>Maglocks, stairwell re-entry, doors that must release on fire alarm</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fail-secure</strong></td><td>Locked</td><td>Unlocked</td><td>Perimeter doors, fire-rated doors, most strikes and locksets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Describes the <strong>secure side</strong> only. The egress side is free either way on a properly installed door. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li>Fire-rated doors: fail-secure electrified hardware, listed for fire doors. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80)</span></li><li>Many strikes and locksets are field-convertible. Check the label or the jumper before you blame the controller.</li></ul>"},{"id":"lock-types-compared","title":"Lock types compared","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lock</th><th>Where it goes</th><th>Egress</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Electric strike</strong></td><td>Frame, replaces the strike plate</td><td>Door's own lever</td><td>Must match the latch; watch preload</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rim strike</strong></td><td>Surface of frame</td><td>Exit device</td><td>For rim panic bars</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Maglock</strong></td><td>Frame header, armature on door</td><td>Needs sensor release or hardware release (Reference: <a href=\"#/access/reference/maglock-egress-two-code-paths\"><em>Maglock egress: two code paths</em></a>)</td><td>Fail-safe only; many AHJs restrict</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Electrified lockset</strong></td><td>In the door</td><td>Inside lever always free</td><td>Needs power transfer into the door</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Electrified exit device</strong></td><td>On the door</td><td>Push bar always free</td><td>Latch retraction or electrified trim</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wireless / offline lock</strong></td><td>In the door</td><td>Inside lever always free</td><td>Battery powered; no door wiring</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"typical-lock-and-device-current","title":"Typical lock and device current","html":"<p>Typical values for planning only. <strong>Use the spec sheet</strong> for the actual device. <span class=\"src\">(Typical spec sheets)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>At 12 VDC</th><th>At 24 VDC</th><th>Draws</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maglock, 600 lb</td><td>≈ 500 mA</td><td>≈ 250 mA</td><td>Continuously while locked</td></tr><tr><td>Maglock, 1,200 lb</td><td>≈ 500 mA</td><td>≈ 250 mA</td><td>Continuously while locked</td></tr><tr><td>Electric strike</td><td>≈ 200 to 450 mA</td><td>≈ 100 to 250 mA</td><td>While unlocked (fail-secure)</td></tr><tr><td>Electrified lockset (solenoid)</td><td>≈ 250 to 500 mA</td><td>≈ 150 to 250 mA</td><td>While energized</td></tr><tr><td>Exit device, solenoid latch retraction</td><td>Inrush of several amps</td><td></td><td>Needs a supply made for it <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Card reader</td><td>≈ 50 to 250 mA</td><td></td><td>Continuously</td></tr><tr><td>Motion REX</td><td>≈ 15 to 30 mA</td><td></td><td>Continuously</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"wiegand-reader-wiring","title":"Wiegand reader wiring","html":"<p>Most common color convention. <strong>Check the reader's manual.</strong> <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer convention)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Color</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red</td><td>+ power (usually 12 VDC)</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td>Ground</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>Data 0 (D0)</td></tr><tr><td>White</td><td>Data 1 (D1)</td></tr><tr><td>Brown / orange</td><td>LED control</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Beeper</td></tr><tr><td>Drain</td><td>Shield, grounded at controller only <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>D0 and D1 idle at about 5 V and pulse low.</li><li>Max run about <strong>500 ft</strong> on 22 AWG shielded. <span class=\"src\">(SIA AC-01; manufacturer manuals)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"wiegand-26-bit-format","title":"Wiegand 26-bit format","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bit 1</th><th>Bits 2 to 9</th><th>Bits 10 to 25</th><th>Bit 26</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Even parity (bits 2 to 13)</td><td>Facility code 0 to 255</td><td>Card number 0 to 65,535</td><td>Odd parity (bits 14 to 25)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><span class=\"src\">(26-bit Wiegand format)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Printed card numbers are often &quot;FC-card&quot; (for example 123-45678) or a long decimal of the whole number. Know which before you type one in.</li><li>Other formats: 34, 35, 37 bit and proprietary. The controller must match the cards.</li></ul>"},{"id":"osdp-quick-facts","title":"OSDP quick facts","html":"<ul><li>RS-485, 2 data wires (A/B) plus power and ground; twisted pair for data. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></li><li>Daisy chain (multi-drop), each reader with its own address; no star wiring. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></li><li>Up to about <strong>4,000 ft</strong> of RS-485 cable. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP; TIA-485)</span></li><li>120 Ω termination at both ends of long runs. <span class=\"src\">(TIA-485; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li>Default baud rate commonly 9600; address, baud, and keys must match the controller.</li><li><strong>Secure Channel</strong> (AES-128) encrypts the link. Turn it on. <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP)</span></li><li>Supervised: the controller reports a reader that stops answering.</li></ul>"},{"id":"cable-and-distance-limits","title":"Cable and distance limits","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Limit</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wiegand reader</td><td>≈ 500 ft</td><td>22 AWG shielded <span class=\"src\">(SIA AC-01; manufacturer manuals)</span></td></tr><tr><td>OSDP / RS-485</td><td>≈ 4,000 ft</td><td>Twisted pair <span class=\"src\">(SIA OSDP; TIA-485)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ethernet / PoE</td><td>100 m (328 ft)</td><td>Per channel, switch to device <span class=\"src\">(TIA-568)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Lock power</td><td>Voltage drop decides</td><td>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/drop\">Voltage Drop calculator</a></strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reader power</td><td>Voltage drop decides</td><td>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/reader\">Reader Cable calculator</a></strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Cable ratings:</strong> CL2 general, CL2R riser, CL2P plenum (CM types may substitute). <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725 / 722)</span> <strong>Support:</strong> from the structure, not ceiling tiles, grid wires, or pipes. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725.24; 300.11)</span> <strong>Separation:</strong> Class 2 not in the same raceway or box as 120 V without a listed barrier. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 725.136)</span></p>"},{"id":"suppression-diode","title":"Suppression diode","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>        LOCK\n   +  ──┬────┬──  from + output\n        │ ▲  │\n     coil │band (cathode) to +\n        │ │  │\n   −  ──┴────┴──  from − output</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>DC locks: a 1N4001 to 1N4007 diode across the lock terminals, <strong>band to +</strong>, <strong>at the lock</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></li><li>Backward = dead short across the lock output.</li><li>AC locks: MOV instead of a diode.</li><li>Skip it if the lock has built-in suppression.</li></ul>"},{"id":"power-supply-and-battery-quick-rules","title":"Power supply and battery quick rules","html":"<ul><li>Listed for access control (UL 294) or burglar alarm (UL 603) use. <span class=\"src\">(UL 294; UL 603)</span></li><li>Load to no more than about <strong>80%</strong> of the rating. <span class=\"src\">(Design practice)</span></li><li>Separate lock power from controller and reader power.</li><li>Battery: <strong>Required Ah = load A × standby h × 1.2</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(Industry practice)</span></li><li>UL 294 standby levels: I none, II 30 min, III 2 h, IV 4 h. <span class=\"src\">(UL 294)</span></li><li>24 V = two 12 V batteries in series, same size and age.</li><li>Replace sealed lead-acid batteries every 3 to 5 years; date them. <span class=\"src\">(Battery manufacturers)</span></li><li>PoE: about 15.4 W per port (12.95 W at the device); PoE+ 30 W (25.5 W). <span class=\"src\">(IEEE 802.3af/at)</span></li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/lockpsu\">Access Power calculator</a></strong>.</p>"},{"id":"meter-checks-at-the-door","title":"Meter checks at the door","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Where</th><th>Expect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lock voltage</td><td>At the lock terminals, <strong>lock energized</strong></td><td>Within the lock's rating (often ±10% of 12 or 24 V)</td></tr><tr><td>Reader power</td><td>At the reader, red to black</td><td>Within the reader's rating (often about 12 V)</td></tr><tr><td>Wiegand data</td><td>D0 to ground, D1 to ground, idle</td><td>About 5 V each; pulses low when a card reads</td></tr><tr><td>DPS</td><td>Disconnected, door closed</td><td>Near 0 Ω (closed); OL with door open</td></tr><tr><td>REX contact</td><td>Disconnected</td><td>Changes state when you walk up to the door from inside</td></tr><tr><td>Fire alarm interface</td><td>Power supply FA input</td><td>Closed in normal (most supplies); opens on alarm</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A lock that reads full voltage with no load can still be starved under load. Always measure energized.</p>"},{"id":"egress-door-rules","title":"Egress door rules","html":"<ul><li>Opens from the egress side without a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li>One releasing operation. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li>Hardware 34 to 48 in above the floor. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; ADA 404.2.7)</span></li><li>One hand, no tight grasping, pinching, or twisting; 5 lbf max to operate. <span class=\"src\">(ADA 309.4)</span></li><li>Interior door opening force 5 lbf max (fire doors: AHJ minimum). <span class=\"src\">(ADA 404.2.9)</span></li><li>Readers and keypads within 15 to 48 in reach range. <span class=\"src\">(ADA 308)</span></li><li>Panic hardware on assembly and educational doors serving 50 or more, high hazard, and certain electrical rooms; bar at least half the door width. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"maglock-egress-two-code-paths","title":"Maglock egress: two code paths","html":"<p><strong>Path 1: Sensor release</strong> <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.2)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Egress-side motion sensor unlocks the door; loss of sensor power unlocks</li><li>Loss of lock power unlocks</li><li><strong>PUSH TO EXIT</strong> button 40 to 48 in high, within 5 ft, cuts lock power directly, unlocked at least 30 s <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.2)</span></li><li>Fire alarm or sprinkler activation unlocks until reset <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>UL 294 listed <span class=\"src\">(UL 294)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Path 2: Door hardware release</strong> <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Lever or panic bar with a built-in switch that directly cuts lock power</li><li>Obvious operation, one hand</li><li>Loss of power unlocks</li><li>Panic hardware (where required) releases the lock</li></ul>\n<p>Get AHJ approval first; many restrict maglocks.</p>"},{"id":"delayed-egress","title":"Delayed egress","html":"<ul><li>Only in occupancies the code allows, in fully sprinklered or fully detected buildings. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>≤ 15 lbf for ≤ 3 s starts an irreversible release; unlocks within <strong>15 s</strong> (30 s if AHJ approves); local alarm sounds. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>Rearms manually at the door only. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>Releases immediately on sprinkler or detection activation, power loss, and from the fire command center. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>Sign within 12 in above the hardware: &quot;PUSH UNTIL ALARM SOUNDS. DOOR CAN BE OPENED IN 15 SECONDS.&quot; Letters 1 in high, 1/8 in stroke. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li><li>No more than one delayed egress door in the path to an exit. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.1)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"fire-alarm-release","title":"Fire alarm release","html":"<ul><li>Fail-safe locks on egress doors unlock on fire alarm and stay unlocked until reset. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>Fire alarm contact in the <strong>lock power path</strong> (power supply FA input), not a software input. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>Fire alarm relay within 3 ft of the controlled device, wiring to the relay supervised. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>Stairway doors locked from the stair side unlock for re-entry. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 7.2.1.5)</span></li><li>The fire alarm contractor owns the relay; coordinate and test together. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Test every fail-safe door at acceptance and after changes, with the fire alarm account on test. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"fire-rated-doors","title":"Fire-rated doors","html":"<ul><li>Label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame.</li><li>Must positively latch: electrified hardware listed for fire doors; strikes fail-secure. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80)</span></li><li>No field modification beyond what NFPA 80 allows. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80 Ch. 4)</span></li><li>Closer stays; no unlisted hold-opens.</li><li>Inspected and tested every year, with records. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 80 Ch. 5; NFPA 101 7.2.1.15)</span></li><li>Penetrations in rated walls get a listed firestop system. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 7; NEC 300.21)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"door-test-checklist","title":"Door test checklist","html":"<ul class=\"checklist\"><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Valid card: unlocks, relocks, logged with the right name and door</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Invalid and off-schedule card: denied and logged</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>REX trips from inside, not from outside</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Forced door and held-open alarms report and restore</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Inside hardware opens the door in every lock state, power off included</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>AC off: runs on battery; AC and battery off: fail-safe unlocks, fail-secure stays locked outside</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Fire alarm release (fail-safe egress doors), account on test <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Lock voltage at the lock, energized</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Reader and enclosure tampers report</span></label></li></ul>"},{"id":"glossary","title":"Glossary","html":"<ul><li><strong>Access level (access group):</strong> a set of doors and schedules given to cardholders.</li><li><strong>Anti-passback:</strong> a card must exit before it can enter again.</li><li><strong>Armature:</strong> the steel plate on the door that a maglock holds.</li><li><strong>Backset:</strong> distance from the door edge to the center of the lock's hub.</li><li><strong>Credential:</strong> card, fob, phone, PIN, or biometric that identifies a person.</li><li><strong>Delayed egress:</strong> a lock that delays opening an exit door by 15 or 30 seconds, under strict code rules.</li><li><strong>DPS:</strong> door position switch; tells the controller the door is closed.</li><li><strong>Edge controller:</strong> a single-door controller at the door, often powered by PoE.</li><li><strong>Electrified hinge:</strong> a hinge with wires through it to power hardware in the door.</li><li><strong>Facility code:</strong> the site number in a Wiegand card number.</li><li><strong>Fail-safe:</strong> unlocked when power is off.</li><li><strong>Fail-secure:</strong> locked when power is off.</li><li><strong>First-person-in:</strong> a scheduled unlock waits for the first valid card.</li><li><strong>Forced door:</strong> the door opened with no valid card or REX.</li><li><strong>Handing:</strong> which side the hinges are on and which way the door swings.</li><li><strong>Held open:</strong> the door stayed open past the allowed time.</li><li><strong>Keeper:</strong> the part of an electric strike that swings free to release the latch.</li><li><strong>Maglock:</strong> electromagnetic lock.</li><li><strong>MOV:</strong> metal oxide varistor; suppresses spikes on AC locks.</li><li><strong>OSDP:</strong> Open Supervised Device Protocol; encrypted, supervised reader wiring over RS-485.</li><li><strong>Preload:</strong> door pressure on the latch that can bind a strike.</li><li><strong>REX:</strong> request to exit.</li><li><strong>RS-485:</strong> two-wire data bus used by OSDP and many controller networks.</li><li><strong>Sensor release:</strong> code path for a maglock released by an egress-side motion sensor with a push button backup.</li><li><strong>Shunt:</strong> ignoring the door contact during a valid entry or exit.</li><li><strong>Wiegand:</strong> older, unencrypted one-way reader wiring.</li></ul>"}]},"troubleshoot":{"introHtml":"<p>One guide per common access service call, for the Troubleshoot tab. Each guide is a step-by-step flowchart: do the step, then follow the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Every guide starts the same way:</strong> tell the building contact what you're doing, put monitored door alarms on test, and make sure the door you're working on can always be opened from the egress side while you work. <span class=\"src\">(Industry practice)</span> <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 101 Ch. 7)</span> Anything that touches the fire alarm release gets the fire alarm contractor and the fire alarm account on test first. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></p>","guides":[{"num":1,"title":"Valid card reads, door doesn't unlock","symptomHtml":"Reader beeps and flashes green (or the log shows access granted), but the door stays locked.","html":"<ol><li>Check the event log. Does it show <strong>granted</strong> for that card at that door? No → it's a programming or card issue, go to <a href=\"#/access/troubleshoot/3\">Guide 3</a>.</li><li>Listen and feel at the lock while someone badges. Does the strike or lock click?<ul><li>Clicks but door won't open → mechanical: go to step 6.</li><li>No click → electrical: go to step 3.</li></ul></li><li>Meter the lock output at the controller or power supply while badging. Voltage switches on (or off, for fail-safe) → the controller side is fine; go to step 4. Nothing changes → check the relay programming (fail-safe vs fail-secure setting), the relay's wiring (NO vs NC contact), and the output fuse or PTC on the power supply.</li><li>Meter <strong>at the lock, energized</strong> (Reference: <a href=\"#/access/reference/meter-checks-at-the-door\"><em>Meter checks at the door</em></a>). Low voltage → voltage drop, a loose splice, or a thin power transfer hinge. Use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/drop\">Voltage Drop calculator</a></strong>.</li><li>Correct voltage at the lock but no action → lock set for the wrong voltage, wrong fail mode, or a failed coil. Check the jumper and the label.</li><li><strong>Strike clicks but door won't open:</strong> preload. Push the door closed firmly and badge again. Opens → the door is pushing on the keeper (weatherstrip, warped door, closer adjustment, air pressure). Fix the door alignment or use a high-preload strike.</li><li>Check that the lockset's latch lands in the keeper, and that the deadlatch isn't falling into the keeper gap.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the quickest way you prove preload on a strike.</p></aside>"},{"num":2,"title":"Reader dead: no LED, no beep","symptomHtml":"Reader shows no light and doesn't respond to cards.","html":"<ol><li>Is the controller online and are other readers on it working? Not → go to <a href=\"#/access/troubleshoot/9\">Guide 9</a> (it may be power for the whole panel).</li><li>Meter reader power <strong>at the reader</strong>, red to black. About 12 V (or the reader's rating) → go to step 4. Zero → step 3. Low → voltage drop or overload; use the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/reader\">Reader Cable calculator</a></strong>.</li><li>Meter at the controller's reader terminals. Voltage there but not at the reader → open cable or bad splice. No voltage at the controller → reader power fuse or PTC tripped (often from a short in the reader cable). Disconnect the reader and see if it recovers.</li><li>Power is good but reader is dead → failed reader, or a reader that needs to be configured (OSDP address or mode). Swap with a known-good reader of the same model.</li><li>Outdoors: look for water in the back box, corroded terminals, or vandalism.</li></ol>"},{"num":3,"title":"Card denied, or reads the wrong number","symptomHtml":"A cardholder who should have access is denied, or the log shows a different card number than expected.","html":"<ol><li>Read the denial reason in the log:<ul><li><strong>Unknown card</strong> → card not in the system, or the number doesn't match. Go to step 3.</li><li><strong>Wrong time / schedule</strong> → check the access level's schedule and holidays.</li><li><strong>Wrong door / no access</strong> → check the cardholder's access levels include this door.</li><li><strong>Expired / disabled</strong> → check the card's activation and expiration dates.</li></ul></li><li>Check the <strong>controller's clock and time zone</strong>. A controller off by an hour (daylight saving) denies cards at the edges of schedules.</li><li><strong>Unknown card:</strong> compare the number in the log with the number in the cardholder record. Facility code wrong → card enrolled with the wrong format or facility code. Number completely different every read → Wiegand D0 and D1 swapped, or the wrong format set on the reader port.</li><li>Changes in the software haven't reached the controller → check that the controller is online and the database download finished.</li><li>One card fails everywhere, others work → damaged card or a card technology the readers don't read.</li></ol>"},{"num":4,"title":"False forced-door alarms","symptomHtml":"Door forced open alarms with no break-in.","html":"<ol><li>Look at the pattern in the log. Forced alarm right after a valid card → the DPS is seeing the door open after the unlock time ended, or relock-on-close isn't set. Forced alarm when people walk out → REX problem.</li><li><strong>REX:</strong> walk up to the door from inside. Does the REX input change state before your hand reaches the hardware? No → re-aim the motion REX toward the door hardware, or use a hardware REX. A REX that trips too late is the most common cause.</li><li><strong>DPS:</strong> open and close the door while watching the input. Bounces open and closed (door rattles, wind, air pressure) → adjust the DPS, increase the gap tolerance, or fix the door. Misaligned magnet → realign.</li><li><strong>Door doesn't latch:</strong> a door that pops open on air pressure or a weak closer shows as forced. Fix the closer and latching.</li><li><strong>Mechanical key override:</strong> someone is using a key. That's correctly reported as forced unless the lock reports key use.</li><li>Check unlock time: too short for slow users → extend it or give them extended unlock.</li></ol>"},{"num":5,"title":"Held-open alarms","symptomHtml":"Door held open alarms when the door looks closed, or too many held-open alarms.","html":"<ol><li>Is the door actually closed and latched? No → closer adjustment, a sweep or threshold dragging, or a propped door (talk to the customer).</li><li>Door is closed but the DPS reads open → DPS gap too large, magnet missing or misaligned, broken wire. Meter the DPS disconnected: near 0 Ω with the door closed, open with it open (Reference: <a href=\"#/access/reference/meter-checks-at-the-door\"><em>Meter checks at the door</em></a>).</li><li>Doors that legitimately stay open (deliveries, loading docks) → lengthen the held-open time or put the door on a schedule, with the customer's approval.</li><li>Pre-alarm beeping without anyone noticing → move the reader beeper or add a local sounder.</li></ol>"},{"num":6,"title":"Maglock won't hold, or holds weakly","symptomHtml":"Door can be pulled open while the maglock should be locked.","html":"<ol><li>Check the bond sensor or status LED on the maglock, if it has one. Not bonded → step 2.</li><li><strong>Voltage at the maglock, energized.</strong> Low → voltage drop or wrong voltage setting; a 24 V-set maglock on 12 V holds weakly.</li><li><strong>Armature:</strong> it must float on its rubber washers. Over-tightened, it can't sit flat. Loosen to factory spec.</li><li><strong>Alignment:</strong> the armature should meet the magnet face fully. A door that sags or a bent bracket leaves a gap. Even paper or paint on the faces cuts holding force a lot.</li><li><strong>Faces:</strong> clean rust or debris off both faces; don't grind them.</li><li>Door opens on REX, push button, or fire alarm when it shouldn't → check those inputs aren't stuck (a failed REX holding the lock released, or a fire alarm relay in alarm).</li></ol>"},{"num":7,"title":"Electric strike won't release, or releases intermittently","symptomHtml":"Card grants and the strike clicks (or should), but the door stays latched some or all of the time.","html":"<ol><li><strong>Preload test:</strong> push the door closed hard, then badge. Works only when pushed → preload. Fix the door, adjust the strike position, or use a high-preload strike.</li><li><strong>Voltage at the strike, energized.</strong> Low → voltage drop or a weak power transfer. Correct → step 3.</li><li><strong>Fit:</strong> the latch must sit fully in the keeper pocket with room to move. A latch that rides on the keeper edge, or a deadlatch in the keeper gap, binds.</li><li><strong>Wrong mode:</strong> a fail-safe strike on a fail-secure output (or the reverse) does the opposite of what you expect.</li><li><strong>Continuous duty:</strong> a strike held unlocked for hours on a schedule must be rated for continuous duty; an intermittent strike overheats and fails.</li><li>Intermittent on cold days → door shrinks or swells; adjust the strike and check the closer.</li></ol>"},{"num":8,"title":"Door won't relock, or stays unlocked","symptomHtml":"Door stays unlocked after the unlock time, or is unlocked when it should be locked.","html":"<ol><li>Check the door's mode and schedule in the software. Is it on a scheduled unlock, a manual override, or a lockdown release someone forgot to undo?</li><li><strong>REX stuck active</strong> (motion REX seeing a heater, a sign moving in air, or a door gap) and REX set to unlock → the lock keeps cycling open. Watch the REX input.</li><li><strong>Fire alarm release still active</strong> → the fire alarm panel isn't reset or its relay is stuck. Check with the fire alarm contractor. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li><strong>Fail-safe lock with no power</strong> → check the power supply output, fuse, and battery (<a href=\"#/access/troubleshoot/11\">Guide 11</a>).</li><li>Relay welded closed on the controller → swap to a spare relay or replace the module, and add a diode at the lock so it doesn't happen again.</li><li>Strike held mechanically → a latch with a hold-back (dogged exit device) or a taped latch. Not an electrical problem.</li></ol>"},{"num":9,"title":"Controller offline or communication lost","symptomHtml":"Software shows a controller, door module, or reader offline.","html":"<ol><li>Is it one controller, a group, or everything? Everything → the server, software service, or network on the server side.</li><li><strong>One IP controller:</strong> does it have power and a link light at its network port? Ping it from a workstation on the same network. No reply → cable, switch port, VLAN change, or an IP address conflict. Ask IT what changed.</li><li><strong>PoE controller:</strong> check the switch's PoE budget and port status. A switch that's out of PoE budget turns off ports.</li><li><strong>RS-485 door modules:</strong> check the bus wiring (A/B polarity, termination at the ends, shield grounded once), addresses (two modules on the same address), and the baud rate.</li><li><strong>OSDP reader offline:</strong> address, baud, and Secure Channel key must match. A reader replaced with a new one needs to be re-keyed.</li><li>Controller online but doors not working while offline → check that it has its database (some controllers lose it after a power-down with a dead backup battery).</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the first question you ask IT when a controller drops off.</p></aside>"},{"num":10,"title":"Doors didn't release on fire alarm (or won't relock after reset)","symptomHtml":"Fail-safe doors stayed locked during a fire alarm or a test, or stay unlocked after the fire alarm is reset.","html":"<p><strong>This is a life safety failure. Tell the building owner right away, and keep the doors unlocked or posted until it's fixed.</strong> <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></p>\n<ol><li>Get the fire alarm contractor and put the fire alarm account on test. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Activate the release and meter the fire alarm interface input at the access power supply. Does the contact change state? No → the fire alarm relay isn't operating (programming or wiring on the fire alarm side).</li><li>Contact changes but locks stay powered → the FA input is jumpered, wired to the wrong terminals, or the locks are on an output the FA input doesn't control (a second supply, or the controller's own lock output). Every fail-safe egress lock must lose power on alarm.</li><li>A push button or REX wired as a bypass around the release → rewire so the fire alarm contact cuts lock power directly. <span class=\"src\">(IBC Ch. 10; NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li><strong>Won't relock after reset:</strong> the FA relay is still in alarm or not reset, or the power supply has a latching FA input that needs its own reset. Check the supply's FA input setting.</li><li>Retest every door the release controls, and record the test. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li></ol>"},{"num":11,"title":"Power supply troubles: AC loss, low battery, blown output","symptomHtml":"AC fail or low battery reported, or one or more doors lost power.","html":"<ol><li><strong>AC fail:</strong> check the breaker and the AC input at the supply's terminals. Breaker on but no AC → the circuit or a switched outlet. Label the breaker so it doesn't happen again.</li><li><strong>Low battery:</strong> with AC on, meter the battery. Disconnect it and meter the charger output (often about 13.6 V for a 12 V system). Charger good, battery low after a day of charging → replace the batteries (both in a 24 V pair). Date the new ones. <span class=\"src\">(Battery manufacturers)</span></li><li><strong>Output dead:</strong> find the fuse or PTC for that output. Blown or tripped → disconnect the load and look for a short: a pinched cable at the door, a backward diode, or a failed lock.</li><li><strong>Overloaded:</strong> add up the load with the <strong><a href=\"#/access/calculators/lockpsu\">Access Power calculator</a></strong>. Over about 80% of the rating, or batteries that can't last the required standby → add a supply. <span class=\"src\">(Design practice)</span></li><li>Supply cycling or hot → overload or a failing supply.</li></ol>"},{"num":12,"title":"Controller resets or readers glitch when a lock operates","symptomHtml":"The controller reboots, a reader beeps oddly, or the wrong number reads when a door unlocks or relocks.","html":"<ol><li>Does it happen exactly when the lock changes state? Yes → lock kickback or inrush.</li><li>Check for a <strong>suppression diode at the lock</strong> (or built-in suppression). Missing → add one at the lock, band to +. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></li><li>Is the lock powered from the <strong>same supply or output</strong> as the controller or readers? Yes → move locks to a separate supply or output.</li><li>Solenoid latch retraction exit device → needs a supply made for its inrush. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer instructions)</span></li><li>Lock and reader cables bundled together over a long run, with the reader shield not grounded → ground the shield at the controller end and separate the cables where you can. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li>Recheck after the fix by cycling the lock twenty times while watching the controller and reader.</li></ol>"}]},"search":[{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.1 What access control does","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.1","text":"An access control system decides who can go through which door at what time , and keeps a record of every attempt. It replaces keys with credentials (cards, fobs, phones, PINs) that can be added, removed, or limited from software. When an employee leaves, you delete their card instead of rekeying the building. Three jobs, all at once: - Keep people out who shouldn't be there (the secure side of the door). - Let people out freely, every time, even when the power or the system fails (the egress side of the door). This is the job that's regulated by building and life safety codes. - Report what happened: who badged in, which doors were forced or propped open, which doors are offline. Access control isn't a burglar alarm, but the two often work together. The access system may arm and disarm the intrusion panel, and door alarms often report to the same monitoring center."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.2 Parts of an access-controlled door","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.2","text":"Almost every access door has the same building blocks. Part What it does Where it lives Credential Card, fob, phone, or PIN that identifies the person In the user's pocket Reader Reads the credential and sends the number to the controller Secure side, next to the door Controller Decides yes or no, drives the lock, logs the event Secure area: IT closet, above ceiling, or at the door (edge controller) Electrified lock Electric strike, magnetic lock, electrified lockset, or electrified exit device In or on the door and frame Door position switch (DPS) Tells the controller whether the door is closed Top of the door frame, usually recessed Request to exit (REX) Tells the controller someone is leaving, so opening the door isn't a forced-door alarm Egress side: motion sensor above the door, or a switch in the exit hardware Power supply Powers the locks (and often the controller and readers) with battery backup Next to the controller Software Where cardholders, access levels, schedules, and reports live Server, workstation, or cloud You'll learn each part in detail in Modules 3, 4, and 5."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.3 Fail-safe and fail-secure","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.3","text":"These two words come up on every door. They describe what the lock does on the secure side when it loses power . Term Power off Power on Typical devices Fail-safe Unlocked Locked Magnetic locks; some electric strikes and locksets set fail-safe Fail-secure Locked Unlocked Most electric strikes; most electrified locksets and exit devices Memory aid: fail- safe keeps people safe (door opens); fail- secure keeps the building secure (door stays locked). The egress side is always free. On a properly installed door, someone inside can always get out with one motion, whatever the lock does on the secure side. A fail-secure strike stays locked from the outside during a power failure, but the lever or panic bar on the inside still opens the door mechanically. A magnetic lock has no mechanical way to release it. It holds only while powered, so it must be fail-safe, and the code adds extra rules to guarantee people can get out (Module 8). Which one to use depends on the door: - Fire-rated doors must stay latched in a fire, so their electrified hardware is fail-secure. - Stairwell doors that are locked from the stair side often have to unlock on a fire alarm so people can get back onto a floor (Module 8). - Perimeter doors are usually fail-secure so a power failure doesn't leave the building open. Field tip (David to add): how you tell fail-safe from fail-secure on a strike that's already installed."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.4 Power and wiring basics for access","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.4","text":"If you've done intrusion work, the electrical basics are the same: Ohm's law, voltage drop, series and parallel (see the Intrusion pack's Foundations module). What's different is the load : locks draw a lot more current than motion detectors. - Locks run on 12 VDC or 24 VDC . Many are field-selectable with a jumper or by wiring. Setting a 12 V lock on 24 V will burn out its coil; setting a 24 V lock on 12 V gives a weak hold or no release. - A magnetic lock commonly draws about 0.5 A at 12 V (half that at 24 V), all the time it's locked. An electric strike draws a few hundred milliamps, only while unlocked. - Most access power is Class 2 : the supply is limited (no more than 30 V and 100 VA per output for the voltages we use) so the wiring doesn't present a fire or shock hazard. - Class 2 wiring stays separated from 120 V wiring : not in the same raceway, box, or enclosure compartment unless a listed barrier separates them. - A lock is a coil. When its power is cut, the collapsing magnetic field kicks back a voltage spike that can reset or damage the controller. A suppression diode at the lock stops it (Module 5)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.5 Safety and the life safety mindset","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.5","text":"Access control is security equipment installed on exit doors . A mistake can trap people in a fire. Treat every door as part of the building's life safety system. - Never leave a door unable to open from the egress side , even for a few minutes while you work. If you have to take hardware off, prop the door, post someone at it, or leave it unlocked, and tell the building contact. - Tell the building before you work. People may get locked out, doors may go unlocked, and forced and held-open alarms may report to a monitoring center. Put the account on test if door alarms are monitored. - Don't touch the fire alarm release without the fire alarm contractor. The connection that unlocks doors on a fire alarm belongs to both systems. Testing it means putting the fire alarm account on test and following its testing rules (see the Fire pack's testing module). - Ladders and doors: a door swinging into your ladder is the classic access injury. Lock the door open or post someone at it while you work overhead. - Lock out 120 V before working on a power supply's AC input, and disconnect the battery before you change fuses or outputs. - Drilling doors and frames: find out if the door is fire-rated (label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame's hinge rabbet) before you drill anything (Module 4)."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 1: Foundations","where":"Learn","href":"learn/1","text":"Who it's for: techs new to access control, including intrusion and fire techs crossing over. You'll be able to: name every part of an access-controlled door, explain fail-safe and fail-secure, describe the power and wiring an access door uses, and work on doors without trapping or locking out anyone."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.1 One badge read, step by step","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.1","text":"Everything an access system does is built on this sequence. 1. Present. The user holds a card or phone to the reader. 2. Read. The reader pulls the credential number and sends it to the controller (over Wiegand or OSDP wiring, Module 3). 3. Decide. The controller looks the number up in its local database : is the card valid, is it allowed at this door, is it allowed right now? 4. Grant. If yes, the controller energizes (or de-energizes) its lock relay for the unlock time (commonly a few seconds), turns the reader LED green, and shunts the door alarm so opening the door isn't a forced-door alarm. 5. Open and close. The DPS shows the door open, then closed. The lock relocks when the unlock time ends or when the door closes, depending on how it's programmed. 6. Log. The event (who, which door, when, granted or denied) goes to the software. If the card is denied, the LED shows red, nothing unlocks, and the event is logged as a denial with the reason (unknown card, wrong time, wrong door, expired). The controller decides, not the server. Good controllers keep their own copy of the database, so doors keep working when the network or server is down. Events are stored at the controller and uploaded when it reconnects."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.2 Controller architectures","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.2","text":"You'll see four common layouts in the field. Layout How it works Where you see it Standalone Keypad or reader and lock in one unit at the door, programmed at the door Single doors, small offices, storage rooms Panel-based (centralized) A multi-door controller in a closet, home-run cable to every door Most commercial buildings Edge / PoE A small controller at or above each door, powered by and talking over the network cable Newer buildings, retrofits with good network Cloud-hosted Any of the above, with the software running in the cloud instead of on a local server Growing quickly in small and mid-size sites Panel-based systems usually have a main controller that talks to the software and door interface modules (sub-controllers) that each handle one to four doors. The main controller to module link is often RS-485. Home-run cabling for a panel-based door typically means separate cables for the reader, the lock, the DPS, and the REX, all landing at the controller. Composite cables carry all of them in one jacket (Module 5). Wireless and offline locks are a separate category: battery-powered locksets that read the card at the door and talk to a hub (or get updates from the cards themselves). They save wiring but have their own battery maintenance."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.3 The database: people, access levels, schedules","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.3","text":"Every access system organizes permissions the same way. - Cardholders are the people. Each has one or more credentials . - Doors (and readers) are the hardware. - Schedules (time zones) are blocks of time: \"Weekdays 7 am to 6 pm.\" - Access levels (access groups) tie doors to schedules: \"Main entrance and office suite, weekdays 7 to 6.\" - Each cardholder gets one or more access levels. So you never give a person a door directly; you give them an access level, and the access level gives them doors and times. When a new door is added to a suite, you add it to the access level once and everyone who needs it gets it. Other common features: - Holidays: days when normal schedules don't apply. - Door schedules (auto-unlock): the main entrance unlocks itself during business hours. Good practice is first-person-in : it stays locked until a valid card is used after the scheduled time, so a snow day doesn't leave the building open. - Anti-passback: a card used to enter must be used to exit before it can enter again. Stops one card being passed back to a second person. - Two-person rule, card plus PIN, elevator floors: higher-security options you'll meet on larger sites. Field tip (David to add): the schedule mistake you see most on service calls."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.4 Events, alarms, and integrations","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.4","text":"The system watches each door with the DPS and REX. Event What happened Common cause Access granted / denied A credential was read Normal use Door forced open The door opened with no valid card and no REX Break-in, a REX that didn't trip, a bad DPS, a worn latch that let the door pop open Door held open The door stayed open longer than the held-open time Propped door, slow closer, deliveries Tamper A reader, controller, or enclosure was opened Vandalism, or a tech who didn't put the account on test Offline / comm loss The controller or a reader stopped talking Network, cable, or power problem AC loss / low battery Power supply troubles, if the supply is monitored Breaker, failing battery Integrations you'll meet: - Fire alarm: fail-safe locks on egress doors release when the fire alarm activates. This connection is a life safety function (Module 6 and Module 8). - Intrusion: badging in can disarm an area; the access system can report door alarms to the intrusion panel. - Video: door events pull up camera clips. - Elevators: the reader in the cab controls which floor buttons work. - Intercom and visitor management: remote unlock and visitor badges."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 2: How an Access System Works","where":"Learn","href":"learn/2","text":"You'll be able to: follow a badge read from the reader to the lock and back, explain the main controller architectures, set up the building blocks of a database (people, access levels, schedules), and describe what the system reports."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.1 Credential technologies","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.1","text":"Technology How it works Notes 125 kHz proximity (prox) Card broadcasts a fixed number when the reader's field powers it Still everywhere. Easy to copy with cheap cloners; no encryption 13.56 MHz smart card Card has a chip that answers the reader with encrypted data The modern standard. Several competing families; the reader must support the card's family and keys Mobile credential Phone app talks to the reader over Bluetooth or NFC No cards to print; needs readers that support it PIN User types a code on a keypad Shareable, so usually used with a card (card plus PIN) Biometric Fingerprint, face, or iris Can't be lent out; enrollment and privacy rules apply Long range UHF tags or vehicle tags read from several feet away Parking gates and vehicle entrances Multi-technology readers read both prox and smart cards. They're the usual way to migrate a site: install new readers that read the old prox cards, then reissue cards over time and turn prox off. Card numbers aren't secret on prox. If the customer cares about security, recommend smart cards or mobile with encryption turned on, not just a smart card reading the card's serial number."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.2 Wiegand","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.2","text":"Wiegand is the most common reader-to-controller wiring. It's old, simple, and one-way: the reader sends the card number and the controller sends back LED and beeper signals on separate wires. Wires (the most common color convention; always check the reader's manual): Color Function Red + power (usually 12 VDC) Black Ground Green Data 0 (D0) White Data 1 (D1) Brown or orange LED control Yellow Beeper control Bare / drain Shield D0 and D1 sit at about 5 V when idle and pulse low to send a 0 (on D0) or a 1 (on D1). Swap them and the controller gets the wrong number or nothing at all. The 26-bit format. The classic Wiegand number is 26 bits: Bit 1 Bits 2 to 9 Bits 10 to 25 Bit 26 Even parity Facility code (0 to 255) Card number (0 to 65,535) Odd parity Only 65,536 card numbers per facility code means duplicates between sites are common. Larger formats (34-bit, 35-bit, 37-bit, and proprietary formats) give more numbers. The controller must be set to the format the cards use. Distance: Wiegand is good to about 500 ft using 22 AWG shielded cable. Security: Wiegand is unencrypted and unsupervised. Someone with access to the wires at the reader can capture card numbers or replay them, and the controller can't tell if a reader has been swapped. That's why OSDP is replacing it."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.3 OSDP","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.3","text":"OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol) is the modern reader standard, published by the Security Industry Association. - Two-way. The controller polls the reader; the reader answers. If a reader stops answering, the controller knows right away (it's supervised ). - RS-485 wiring: two data wires (often labeled A/B or +/−) plus power and ground. Four conductors, twisted pair for the data. - Multi-drop: several readers can share one RS-485 run, each with its own address. Wire it as a daisy chain, not a star. - Distance: up to about 4,000 ft on RS-485 with proper twisted pair cable. - Termination: long runs get a 120 Ω terminating resistor at each end of the bus. Short runs often don't need it; follow the controller manual. - Secure Channel encrypts the link (AES-128). Turn it on: OSDP without Secure Channel is supervised but not encrypted. - Settings must match on both ends: address, baud rate (commonly 9600 by default), and Secure Channel keys. Retrofits: OSDP usually runs on the existing Wiegand cable, using one twisted pair for data. Check that the old cable has a twisted pair available. Field tip (David to add): what you check first when an OSDP reader won't come online."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.4 Mounting and wiring the reader","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.4","text":"Height and reach. Card readers and keypads are operable parts, so they go within the accessible reach range: no higher than 48 in and no lower than 15 in above the floor, with clear floor space in front. Many specs call for 42 to 48 in to the center of the reader. Location. - On the latch side of the door, close enough that a user can badge and pull the door in one motion. - On the wall or mullion, not on the door, unless it's a reader built into the lockset. - Metal mullions detune readers and cut read range. Use a mullion-mount reader or a spacer, and test range after mounting. - Keep readers away from each other (back-to-back readers on two sides of a wall can interfere). - Outdoors: a reader rated for the weather, with the cable entry sealed and a drip loop. Cable. Typical Wiegand reader cable is 22 AWG, 6 conductors, shielded (8 conductors if the reader needs more functions). Ground the shield drain at the controller end only ; at the reader, cut it back and tape it so it can't touch anything. Power. Most readers want 12 VDC and draw 50 to 250 mA (more when a heater or LCD is on). Long runs on 22 AWG drop voltage; check with the Reader Cable calculator ."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 3: Credentials and Readers","where":"Learn","href":"learn/3","text":"You'll be able to: tell credential technologies apart, read a Wiegand card number, wire a Wiegand or OSDP reader, and mount a reader where everyone can reach it."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.1 Door anatomy and handing","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.1","text":"Know the names before you order hardware. - Frame: head (top), jambs (sides), stop (the lip the door closes against). Hollow metal frames are steel; aluminum storefront frames are thin-walled tube; wood frames are common in residential and interiors. - Door: hinge edge, lock edge (latch edge), push side, pull side. - Hardware: hinges, lockset or exit device, closer, stop, and the door hardware schedule that lists every piece by door number on commercial jobs. - Handing: stand on the outside (the secure, keyed side). Hinges on the left and the door swings away from you: left hand (LH). Hinges on the right, swinging away: right hand (RH). Swinging toward you: left hand reverse (LHR) or right hand reverse (RHR). Strikes, exit devices, and some maglock brackets are handed. Read the existing hardware first. The lock that's already in the door decides what electric strike will work with it (latch type, backset, and whether it has a deadlatch). Field tip (David to add): the quick way you check handing and backset at a door before ordering."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.2 Electric strikes","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.2","text":"An electric strike replaces the frame's strike plate. When it releases, its keeper swings free so the latch can pass without being retracted. The lockset stays mechanical. - Fail-secure is the most common: locked without power, unlocked when energized. The door's own lever always lets people out, so egress is unaffected. - Fail-safe strikes exist for doors that must unlock on power loss or fire alarm, but not on fire-rated doors (Lesson 4.5). - Match the lock: the strike must fit the latch type (cylindrical, mortise, rim exit device), the latch throw, and the deadlatch. A deadlatch that falls into the keeper gap leaves the door unlockable or unlatched. - Preload: a door that's pushing or pulling on the latch (weatherstrip, warped door, stack pressure) can bind the keeper so it won't release. Strikes are rated for a preload; when it's exceeded, fix the door or use a high-preload strike. - Current: draws only while unlocked, commonly a few hundred milliamps at 12 V. Continuous-duty strikes are needed when a door is held unlocked on a schedule. - Rim strikes go with rim exit devices (panic bars) and mount on the surface of the frame."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.3 Magnetic locks","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.3","text":"A magnetic lock (maglock) is an electromagnet on the frame header and a steel armature plate on the door. Energized, it holds the door shut; de-energized, it lets go. - Holding force is commonly 600 lb (interior) or 1,200 lb (perimeter). Current is commonly about 0.5 A at 12 V or 0.25 A at 24 V, drawn all the time the door is locked . - Always fail-safe. Because it holds with no mechanical release, egress must be guaranteed electrically: the code requires either a motion sensor plus push button arrangement or a switch built into the door hardware (Module 8). - The magnet always mounts on the secure side . On a door that swings away from the secure side (the usual out-swinging exit door), it mounts under the header on the push side, with an L bracket if the frame is narrow. On a door that swings toward the secure side, a Z bracket brings the magnet down to meet the armature on the pull side. - Armature must float on its rubber washers so it can align itself flat with the magnet. A tightened-down armature won't seat flat and holds poorly. - Residual magnetism: a worn or dirty magnet can hold slightly after release. Most maglocks have a built-in suppression and anti-residual circuit; keep faces clean. - Bond sensor and DPS options report whether the magnet is actually holding. Maglocks are easy to install on doors where nothing else fits (glass doors, existing panic hardware), which is why they're overused. Many AHJs restrict them; check before you quote one."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.4 Electrified locksets, exit devices, and power transfer","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.4","text":"- Electrified lockset (cylindrical or mortise): the lock itself has a solenoid or motor that locks or unlocks the outside lever. The inside lever is always free. Fail-secure or fail-safe by order or by setting. Usually the cleanest, most secure option. - Electrified exit device (panic bar): either electric latch retraction (pulls the latch in so the door is push/pull, often used with door operators) or electrified trim (unlocks the outside lever). - Solenoid latch retraction can pull a large inrush current for a fraction of a second (several amps). It needs a power supply or controller made for it. Motorized latch retraction draws much less. - Request-to-exit switch built into the lockset or exit device: reports that the inside hardware was used. Getting power into the door: - Electrified hinge (power transfer hinge): wires run through the hinge. Clean and protected. - Concealed power transfer: a device mortised into the door edge and frame. - Door loop: an armored flexible loop between frame and door. Cheapest, most exposed. All three limit wire count and wire size. Check the conductor gauge in the hinge against the lock's current and the run length (voltage drop, Module 5)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.5 DPS, REX, and fire-rated doors","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.5","text":"Door position switch (DPS). A recessed magnetic contact in the frame head near the latch side (or a surface contact). Most controllers want it closed when the door is closed. Mount it where it will show \"open\" before the door has moved far enough to let someone through. REX. Tells the controller someone's leaving so the door opening isn't reported as forced. - Motion REX: a PIR mounted above the door on the egress side, aimed down at the area in front of the door. Aim it so it can't be tripped from the secure side through the gap under or between doors (people slide things under or use air or smoke through the gap). - Hardware REX: a switch in the lever or exit device. More reliable for reporting. - On a fail-secure strike, the REX usually only shunts the alarm; the lever releases the latch mechanically. - On a maglock, the REX may also be one of the ways the lock is released, under the code rules in Module 8. Fire-rated doors. Look for the label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame. A fire door must positively latch every time it closes, so: - Electrified hardware on it must be listed for use on fire doors , and electric strikes on fire doors must be fail-secure . A fail-safe strike would leave the door unlatched in a fire. - Don't field-modify a fire door or frame (cutting, drilling, enlarging preps) beyond what the fire door standard allows. Most modifications need factory or listed-agency preparation; otherwise the label is void. - Don't add a hold-open or remove the closer to make a door easier to use. Fire doors close and latch."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 4: Locks and Door Hardware","where":"Learn","href":"learn/4","text":"You'll be able to: read a door's handing and hardware, choose between strikes, magnetic locks, electrified locksets, and electrified exit devices, install a DPS and REX that report correctly, and recognize the rules for fire-rated doors."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.1 Access power supplies","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.1","text":"Access power supplies are built for locks: a Class 2 supply with a battery charger and several individually fused or PTC-protected outputs , often with a fire alarm interface input that cuts lock power on alarm. - Listed for the job. Use a power supply listed for access control (UL 294) or burglar alarm (UL 603) use, and for fire alarm use where it's part of a fire alarm system. - Don't load it to 100%. Design for no more than about 80% of the rated output , leaving room for inrush, aging batteries, and the door someone adds next year. - Separate lock power from controller power where you can: a separate supply, or at least separate outputs. A lock's inrush and kickback on the same output as the controller is the classic cause of controllers rebooting when a door unlocks. - 12 or 24 V: pick one per supply and match every lock to it. 24 V halves the current for the same lock, which cuts voltage drop on long runs. - Supervision: wire the supply's AC fail and low battery outputs to controller inputs so power troubles get reported. Use the Access Power calculator to add up the load and pick a supply and battery."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.2 Batteries and standby time","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.2","text":"Access batteries keep doors locked (and readers working) through a power failure. How long is a design decision, not a single code number. - UL 294 standby power levels: Level I has no standby requirement; Level II is 30 minutes; Level III is 2 hours; Level IV is 4 hours. The specification or the customer picks the level. - Formula (same as intrusion): Required Ah = load current in A × standby hours × 1.2. The 1.2 is a 20% margin for battery aging and temperature. - Fail-safe locks drain batteries fast. Every maglock draws current the whole time it's locked. Some designs leave maglocks off the battery on purpose (they unlock in a power failure); that's a security and egress decision for the customer and AHJ, so get it in writing. - Fail-secure strikes barely touch the battery. They draw only while unlocked. - Two 12 V batteries in series for a 24 V supply: same size, same age. - Sealed lead-acid batteries are commonly replaced every 3 to 5 years , and dated when installed."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.3 Suppression and kickback","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.3","text":"When power to a lock coil is cut, the collapsing magnetic field produces a voltage spike in the opposite direction, sometimes hundreds of volts for an instant. It arcs relay contacts, resets controllers, and garbles reader data. - On DC locks , put a diode (commonly a 1N4001 to 1N4007) across the lock terminals, at the lock , reverse biased: the band (cathode) to +, the other end to −. Installed backward, it's a short circuit across the lock output. - Many maglocks and some strikes have built-in suppression . Check before adding a diode. - AC strikes use a MOV (metal oxide varistor) instead, since a diode would block AC. - At the lock is the right place. A diode back at the controller still lets the spike travel the whole cable run next to reader wiring. Field tip (David to add): the symptom that made you start putting a diode on every lock."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.4 Cable","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.4","text":"A typical home-run door, panel-based controller (check the manufacturer's wiring diagram): Device Common cable Reader (Wiegand) 22 AWG, 6 conductor, shielded (8 conductor for extra functions) Reader (OSDP) 22 AWG, 2 twisted pair (data pair plus power pair), shielded Lock 18 AWG, 2 conductor (16 AWG or heavier on long runs or high current) DPS 22 AWG, 2 conductor REX (motion) 22 AWG, 4 conductor (power plus relay contact) Composite cable puts all of these in one jacket and is common on new installs. Cable ratings (NEC power-limited cable types): - CL2 / CL3: general use. - CL2R / CL3R: riser, between floors in a shaft. - CL2P / CL3P: plenum, in air-handling spaces such as return air ceilings. - Communications cable ratings (CM, CMR, CMP) of the same or higher level are allowed substitutes. Support: cable is supported by the building structure with listed hardware, not laid on ceiling tiles or tied to ceiling grid wires, pipes, or conduit. Network cable: Ethernet (Category 5e/6) to IP controllers and PoE devices is limited to 100 m (328 ft) per run."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.5 Voltage drop to the door","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.5","text":"Locks are the heaviest load you'll put on long, thin wire, and a lock that gets too little voltage holds weakly or won't release. Voltage drop = 2 × one-way length × current × Ω per foot Example. A maglock draws 0.5 A at 12 V, 200 ft from the power supply on 18 AWG (0.00639 Ω/ft). Drop = 2 × 200 × 0.5 × 0.00639 = 1.28 V . The lock sees about 10.7 V. Most locks want to see within about 10% of their rated voltage (check the spec sheet); 10.7 V is borderline. The fixes: 16 AWG, set the lock and supply to 24 V (half the current, a quarter of the voltage loss as a percentage), or put a power supply closer to the door. - Measure lock voltage at the lock, with the lock energized . An unloaded reading tells you nothing. - Count the electrified hinge or power transfer: its thin conductors are part of the run. - PoE edge devices: budget the power. A standard PoE port supplies about 15.4 W (about 12.95 W reaches the device); PoE+ supplies 30 W (25.5 W at the device). Locks powered from a PoE controller must fit inside what's left after the controller and reader. Use the Voltage Drop calculator for lock runs and the Reader Cable calculator for readers."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 5: Power and Wiring","where":"Learn","href":"learn/5","text":"You'll be able to: size an access power supply and its batteries, keep lock power from upsetting the controller, pick the right cable for each device, and check voltage drop to the lock and reader."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.1 Door survey","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.1","text":"Most access problems are built in at the survey. Go to every door and write down: - Door and frame: material (hollow metal, aluminum storefront, wood, glass), handing, swing direction, condition. Does it close and latch on its own every time? If not, fix that first; an access system can't secure a door that doesn't latch. - Fire label: on the door edge and in the frame. A fire door limits what hardware you can use and how you install it (Module 4). - Existing hardware: lockset type and brand, exit device, closer, hinges. Take photos of the latch, strike, and both sides of the door. - Egress: is this door part of an exit path? Is panic hardware required? Will the AHJ allow a maglock here? - Cable path: from the door to the controller location. Ceiling type (plenum?), walls (block, drywall), fire-rated walls you'll penetrate. - Power: where the 120 V for the power supply comes from and who provides it. - Network: for IP controllers, a port and an address from the customer's IT. - Fire alarm: does the building have one? Where's the panel, and who services it? Field tip (David to add): the survey question that saves you a return trip."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.2 At the door","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.2","text":"- Reader: latch side, within reach range (Lesson 3.4), on a single-gang box or surface mount. Seal exterior penetrations. - Lock: follow the hardware manufacturer's template exactly. Measure twice on aluminum storefront; you can't undo a hole in a thin-wall frame. - DPS: in the frame head near the latch side, magnet in the door top. Check the gap at the hinge side isn't so big that a door opened a few inches still reads closed. - REX: above the door on the egress side, aimed down at the door hardware area. Walk test it from both sides of the door: it must trip from the inside and must not trip from the outside. - Diode at the lock if the lock doesn't have built-in suppression (Lesson 5.3). - Service loop of a foot or two above the door, so the next tech can remake terminations. - Fire-rated walls: seal every penetration with a listed firestop system for that wall type."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.3 Controller and power supply","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.3","text":"- In a secure location : a locked room, inside the secure perimeter, never on the unsecure side of the door it controls. - Close to the doors it serves to keep cable runs short, but accessible for service (not over a drop ceiling above a desk if you can avoid it). - Enclosure tamper switches wired and reporting. - Dedicated 120 V circuit where possible, not a shared receptacle someone can unplug. Label the breaker. Make the AC connection inside the enclosure's high-voltage compartment, separated from Class 2 wiring. - Grounding: earth ground the enclosure and the controller's ground terminal per the manual. One ground point per shield. - Network: a static address or a DHCP reservation, documented. Ask IT whether the controller is on its own VLAN."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.4 Fire alarm release","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.4","text":"Where a building has a fire alarm system, fail-safe locks on egress doors unlock when the fire alarm activates , and stay unlocked until the fire alarm is reset. How it's wired in practice: - A fire alarm relay (a control module or panel relay) opens on alarm. - That contact goes in the lock power path , typically the access power supply's fire alarm interface input, so the locks lose power directly. Don't rely on a controller input and software to unlock the doors. - The fire alarm relay or control module goes within 3 ft of the device it controls (here, the power supply's fire alarm input), with the fire alarm wiring to the relay supervised, the same rule as any fire alarm control function (see the Fire pack's lesson on control functions). - Fail-secure locks don't unlock on fire alarm unless they're required to (stairwell re-entry, Module 8). Their egress side is already free. - Who wires it: the fire alarm contractor owns the relay and its programming. Coordinate, and test the release together. Test it at acceptance and after any change to either system: put the fire alarm account on test, activate an alarm, confirm every fail-safe door releases, reset, and confirm they relock."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.5 Labeling and documentation","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.5","text":"- Label both ends of every cable with the door number and device (D104-RDR, D104-LOCK). - Label power supply outputs with the doors they feed, and the fuse or PTC size. - Label the breaker for the power supply, and record it inside the enclosure. - Leave a door schedule in the enclosure or with the customer: door number, controller and port, lock type, fail-safe or fail-secure, voltage, power supply output. - Keep the wiring diagram and the controller's IP address and login with the customer's records (not taped to the enclosure)."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 6: Installation","where":"Learn","href":"learn/6","text":"You'll be able to: survey a door before quoting it, mount and wire each device at the door, place the controller and power supply, wire the fire alarm release correctly, and leave a door that the next tech can service."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.1 Bringing hardware online","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.1","text":"Every system differs, but the order is the same. 1. Controllers: give each one its network address (or bus address on RS-485), add it in the software, and confirm it shows online. Update firmware to the version the software expects before you go further. 2. Readers: set the card format (Wiegand) or the OSDP address, baud rate, and Secure Channel. Confirm a test card reads and shows up in the event log, even as \"unknown card.\" 3. Inputs: assign the DPS and REX inputs to each door, and set each as normally open or normally closed (and supervised with resistors if the controller supports it). 4. Outputs: assign the lock relay, and set it for fail-safe or fail-secure so \"unlock\" does the right thing. 5. Power supply supervision: AC fail, low battery, and tamper inputs, named so an operator knows which enclosure has the trouble. Name everything the way the building does: \"D104 Main Lobby Entry,\" not \"Door 1.\" Operators will read these names in alarms at 2 am."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.2 Door timers and modes","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.2","text":"Setting What it does Common starting point Unlock (strike) time How long the lock stays unlocked after a valid card 3 to 5 s Extended unlock Longer unlock for cardholders who need it (ADA) 10 to 30 s Held-open time How long the door can stay open before a held-open alarm 30 to 60 s; longer at loading docks Pre-alarm Reader beeps before held-open alarm A few seconds before held-open Relock on close Lock relocks as soon as the DPS shows closed, rather than waiting out the unlock time On, for most doors REX unlocks Whether REX also unlocks the lock Usually off for fail-secure doors; on for maglock doors per the egress design These are customer choices, not code values; set them with the customer and write them down. Door modes: card only, card plus PIN, unlocked (scheduled), locked (lockdown), first-person-in. Make sure the customer knows how to put a door back to its normal mode after an override."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.3 Schedules, levels, and cardholders","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.3","text":"- Build schedules first, then access levels , then add cardholders (Lesson 2.3). - Holidays go in before the first one arrives. - Least privilege: give each person only the doors and times they need. \"All doors, all times\" is for a few administrators. - Card enrollment: read the card at an enrollment reader or type the number printed on it. Confirm the facility code matches the system's format. - Operators: each person who uses the software gets their own login with only the permissions they need. No shared \"admin\" password. Field tip (David to add): how you handle a customer who wants everyone on \"all doors, all times.\""},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.4 Testing every door","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.4","text":"Test each door from both sides before you call it done. Write the results on a door test sheet. - [ ] Valid card: green LED, lock releases, door opens, relocks on close, event logged with the right name and door - [ ] Invalid card (and a valid card outside its schedule): red LED, stays locked, denial logged - [ ] REX: trips from the inside, does not trip from the outside, opening the door with REX is not a forced alarm - [ ] Forced door: open the door without a card or REX (key override or a helper inside with the REX masked), forced alarm reported - [ ] Held open: prop it past the held-open time, alarm reported, restores when closed - [ ] Egress: inside hardware opens the door with one motion with the lock in every state, including with power off - [ ] Power loss: remove AC, door behaves as designed on battery; remove battery and AC, fail-safe unlocks and fail-secure stays locked from outside - [ ] Fire alarm release (fail-safe egress doors): with the fire alarm account on test, activate an alarm, door releases; reset, door relocks - [ ] Lock voltage at the lock, energized, within the lock's rating - [ ] Tamper on readers and enclosures reports"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.5 Handoff and customer training","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.5","text":"- Train the administrator: adding and removing cardholders, lost cards, changing schedules, holiday setup, running reports, and responding to door alarms. - Lost or terminated cards get disabled right away , not deleted, so their history stays in reports. - Hand over: door schedule, wiring diagrams, controller addresses and admin credentials (in a sealed envelope or password manager, not an email), and the door test sheet. - Explain the egress features to the building: what happens in a fire alarm and a power failure, and that doors must never be chained, padlocked, or blocked. - Inspection: fire door assemblies and certain egress doors need an inspection every year with written records. Tell the customer who does it."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 7: Programming and Commissioning","where":"Learn","href":"learn/7","text":"You'll be able to: set up a door in the software, choose sensible door timers, build schedules and access levels, test every door before handoff, and train the customer."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.1 Who's in charge and which documents apply","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.1","text":"Document What it covers for access control IBC (building code), Chapter 10 Egress doors, locks, electrically locked doors, panic hardware, stairway doors NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) The same topics for buildings that enforce NFPA 101 (often healthcare, schools, and state fire marshal rules) NFPA 80 Fire doors: what hardware can go on them and how they're inspected NFPA 72 Fire alarm, including the relays that release doors NFPA 70 (NEC) Class 2 wiring, cable ratings, power supplies ADA Standards Reach range, hardware height, and operating force for accessible doors UL 294 Listing standard for access control system units (controllers, readers, locks, power supplies) UL 1034, BHMA A156 series Burglary-resistant electric locks; performance grades for strikes, maglocks, and exit devices The AHJ (building official and fire marshal) has the final word. Many require a permit and a plan review for any electrically locked egress door, especially maglocks and delayed egress. Get approval before you install."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.2 Free egress, one operation, and hardware","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.2","text":"The rules every exit door follows, locked or not: - Free egress: an exit door opens from the egress side without a key, a tool, special knowledge, or special effort . - One operation: releasing the door takes one motion. A lever plus a separate deadbolt or a thumb-turn is two operations, which isn't allowed on most exit doors. - Hardware height: door handles, pulls, latches, locks, and other operating parts on accessible doors are 34 to 48 in above the floor. - Operable with one hand , without tight grasping, pinching, or twisting of the wrist, and no more than 5 lbf to operate. - Opening force for interior hinged doors is no more than 5 lbf (fire doors excepted: the minimum the AHJ allows). - Panic hardware is required on exit doors serving assembly and educational occupancies with 50 or more people, high-hazard occupancies, and certain electrical rooms. Its actuating portion extends at least half the door width . An access system almost never changes egress on a door with a mechanical lever or panic bar: the inside hardware always opens the door. The rules in the next lessons apply when the lock itself has no mechanical release (maglocks, fail-safe locksets with no free lever) or when egress is deliberately delayed. Field tip (David to add): the egress violation you find most often on existing access doors."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.3 Sensor release (maglock with motion and push button)","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.3","text":"The most common maglock arrangement: a motion sensor on the egress side unlocks the door as someone approaches, and a push button backs it up. The code calls these sensor release of electrically locked egress doors (older editions: access-controlled egress doors). Requirements: 1. A sensor on the egress side detects someone approaching and unlocks the door. Loss of power to the sensor also unlocks it. 2. Loss of power to the lock unlocks the door. 3. A manual unlock button labeled \"PUSH TO EXIT\" , mounted 40 to 48 in above the floor and within 5 ft of the door. Pressing it cuts power to the lock directly , independent of the access control electronics, and the door stays unlocked for at least 30 seconds . 4. Fire alarm or sprinkler activation (where the building has them) unlocks the doors, and they stay unlocked until the fire alarm is reset. 5. The locking system is listed to UL 294 . Why the push button wiring matters: the button's contact goes in series with the lock's power, usually a timed relay or a pneumatic-delay button, not into a controller input. If the controller locks up, the button still works."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.4 Electromagnetically locked egress doors","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.4","text":"The other code path for a maglock: the door hardware itself releases the magnet. Requirements: 1. The hardware on the door has an obvious method of operation under all lighting conditions and works with one hand . 2. Operating the hardware directly interrupts power to the maglock and unlocks the door immediately (a switch built into the lever or exit device, wired in series with the lock power). 3. Loss of power unlocks the door. 4. Where panic hardware is required, operating the panic hardware releases the maglock. 5. The locking system is listed to UL 294 . No motion sensor or push button is required on this path because the hardware the occupant naturally uses does the release."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.5 Delayed egress and stairway doors","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.5","text":"Delayed egress locks hold an exit door locked for a short delay when someone pushes on it, sounding a local alarm. They're used to stop theft and patient or resident wandering. Only allowed in certain occupancies, and only where the building has a complete sprinkler system or fire detection system . - Applying a force of no more than 15 lbf for no more than 3 s starts an irreversible release; the door unlocks within 15 s (up to 30 s if the AHJ approves). A local audible signal sounds when the process starts. - Once released, it rearms only manually at the door. - It releases immediately on sprinkler or fire detection activation, on loss of power, and from the fire command center where one exists. - A sign on the door, above and within 12 in of the release hardware: \"PUSH UNTIL ALARM SOUNDS. DOOR CAN BE OPENED IN 15 SECONDS.\" (30 where that's approved), in letters at least 1 in high with 1/8 in stroke. - An occupant generally can't be made to pass through more than one delayed egress door before reaching an exit. Stairway doors. In multistory buildings, doors from an exit stair back into a floor generally must allow re-entry . Where they're locked from the stair side, they must unlock automatically on a fire alarm and from the fire command center where there is one (the exact rules depend on building height and the code edition). This is usually fail-safe hardware on the stair side."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.6 NEC, ADA, and inspection","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.6","text":"- NEC: Class 2 power (Lesson 1.4), separation from power wiring, listed cable for the space (CL2, CL2R, CL2P), and support from the structure (Module 5). - ADA: readers within the 15 to 48 in reach range; hardware 34 to 48 in; operable with one hand. - Fire doors (NFPA 80): listed hardware, positive latching, no unapproved field modifications (Module 4). - Annual inspection: fire door assemblies get an inspection and test every year by a qualified person, with a written record. NFPA 101 also requires annual inspection of certain egress doors, including doors with panic hardware and electrically controlled egress. - Documentation: keep the permit, the approved drawings, door test sheets, and the fire alarm release test with the customer's records."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 8: Codes, Egress, and Standards","where":"Learn","href":"learn/8","text":"You'll be able to: name the codes and standards that govern access-controlled doors, apply the free egress rules, and recognize the requirements for sensor release, electromagnetically locked, and delayed egress doors. The building code (most of the US uses the International Building Code, IBC ) and the life safety code ( NFPA 101 ) both regulate locked exit doors, and the jurisdiction decides which edition applies. Section numbers move between editions, so this module names the topics; look up the section in the edition your AHJ has adopted."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Access door at a glance","where":"Reference","href":"reference/access-door-at-a-glance","text":"Device Cable (typical) Controller connection Reader 22/6 shielded (Wiegand) or 22/2 pair + power (OSDP) Reader port Lock 18/2 (heavier on long runs) Lock relay through the power supply DPS 22/2 Input, closed when door is closed REX 22/4 Input; may also cut lock power"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Fail-safe vs fail-secure","where":"Reference","href":"reference/fail-safe-vs-fail-secure","text":"Power off Power on Used for Fail-safe Unlocked Locked Maglocks, stairwell re-entry, doors that must release on fire alarm Fail-secure Locked Unlocked Perimeter doors, fire-rated doors, most strikes and locksets - Describes the secure side only. The egress side is free either way on a properly installed door. - Fire-rated doors: fail-secure electrified hardware, listed for fire doors. - Many strikes and locksets are field-convertible. Check the label or the jumper before you blame the controller."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Lock types compared","where":"Reference","href":"reference/lock-types-compared","text":"Lock Where it goes Egress Notes Electric strike Frame, replaces the strike plate Door's own lever Must match the latch; watch preload Rim strike Surface of frame Exit device For rim panic bars Maglock Frame header, armature on door Needs sensor release or hardware release (Reference: Maglock egress: two code paths ) Fail-safe only; many AHJs restrict Electrified lockset In the door Inside lever always free Needs power transfer into the door Electrified exit device On the door Push bar always free Latch retraction or electrified trim Wireless / offline lock In the door Inside lever always free Battery powered; no door wiring"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Typical lock and device current","where":"Reference","href":"reference/typical-lock-and-device-current","text":"Typical values for planning only. Use the spec sheet for the actual device. Device At 12 VDC At 24 VDC Draws Maglock, 600 lb ≈ 500 mA ≈ 250 mA Continuously while locked Maglock, 1,200 lb ≈ 500 mA ≈ 250 mA Continuously while locked Electric strike ≈ 200 to 450 mA ≈ 100 to 250 mA While unlocked (fail-secure) Electrified lockset (solenoid) ≈ 250 to 500 mA ≈ 150 to 250 mA While energized Exit device, solenoid latch retraction Inrush of several amps Needs a supply made for it Card reader ≈ 50 to 250 mA Continuously Motion REX ≈ 15 to 30 mA Continuously"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Wiegand reader wiring","where":"Reference","href":"reference/wiegand-reader-wiring","text":"Most common color convention. Check the reader's manual. Color Function Red + power (usually 12 VDC) Black Ground Green Data 0 (D0) White Data 1 (D1) Brown / orange LED control Yellow Beeper Drain Shield, grounded at controller only - D0 and D1 idle at about 5 V and pulse low. - Max run about 500 ft on 22 AWG shielded."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Wiegand 26-bit format","where":"Reference","href":"reference/wiegand-26-bit-format","text":"Bit 1 Bits 2 to 9 Bits 10 to 25 Bit 26 Even parity (bits 2 to 13) Facility code 0 to 255 Card number 0 to 65,535 Odd parity (bits 14 to 25) - Printed card numbers are often \"FC-card\" (for example 123-45678) or a long decimal of the whole number. Know which before you type one in. - Other formats: 34, 35, 37 bit and proprietary. The controller must match the cards."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"OSDP quick facts","where":"Reference","href":"reference/osdp-quick-facts","text":"- RS-485, 2 data wires (A/B) plus power and ground; twisted pair for data. - Daisy chain (multi-drop), each reader with its own address; no star wiring. - Up to about 4,000 ft of RS-485 cable. - 120 Ω termination at both ends of long runs. - Default baud rate commonly 9600; address, baud, and keys must match the controller. - Secure Channel (AES-128) encrypts the link. Turn it on. - Supervised: the controller reports a reader that stops answering."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Cable and distance limits","where":"Reference","href":"reference/cable-and-distance-limits","text":"Run Limit Notes Wiegand reader ≈ 500 ft 22 AWG shielded OSDP / RS-485 ≈ 4,000 ft Twisted pair Ethernet / PoE 100 m (328 ft) Per channel, switch to device Lock power Voltage drop decides Use the Voltage Drop calculator Reader power Voltage drop decides Use the Reader Cable calculator Cable ratings: CL2 general, CL2R riser, CL2P plenum (CM types may substitute). Support: from the structure, not ceiling tiles, grid wires, or pipes. Separation: Class 2 not in the same raceway or box as 120 V without a listed barrier."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Suppression diode","where":"Reference","href":"reference/suppression-diode","text":"- DC locks: a 1N4001 to 1N4007 diode across the lock terminals, band to + , at the lock . - Backward = dead short across the lock output. - AC locks: MOV instead of a diode. - Skip it if the lock has built-in suppression."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Power supply and battery quick rules","where":"Reference","href":"reference/power-supply-and-battery-quick-rules","text":"- Listed for access control (UL 294) or burglar alarm (UL 603) use. - Load to no more than about 80% of the rating. - Separate lock power from controller and reader power. - Battery: Required Ah = load A × standby h × 1.2 . - UL 294 standby levels: I none, II 30 min, III 2 h, IV 4 h. - 24 V = two 12 V batteries in series, same size and age. - Replace sealed lead-acid batteries every 3 to 5 years; date them. - PoE: about 15.4 W per port (12.95 W at the device); PoE+ 30 W (25.5 W). Use the Access Power calculator ."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Meter checks at the door","where":"Reference","href":"reference/meter-checks-at-the-door","text":"Check Where Expect Lock voltage At the lock terminals, lock energized Within the lock's rating (often ±10% of 12 or 24 V) Reader power At the reader, red to black Within the reader's rating (often about 12 V) Wiegand data D0 to ground, D1 to ground, idle About 5 V each; pulses low when a card reads DPS Disconnected, door closed Near 0 Ω (closed); OL with door open REX contact Disconnected Changes state when you walk up to the door from inside Fire alarm interface Power supply FA input Closed in normal (most supplies); opens on alarm A lock that reads full voltage with no load can still be starved under load. Always measure energized."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Egress door rules","where":"Reference","href":"reference/egress-door-rules","text":"- Opens from the egress side without a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort. - One releasing operation. - Hardware 34 to 48 in above the floor. - One hand, no tight grasping, pinching, or twisting; 5 lbf max to operate. - Interior door opening force 5 lbf max (fire doors: AHJ minimum). - Readers and keypads within 15 to 48 in reach range. - Panic hardware on assembly and educational doors serving 50 or more, high hazard, and certain electrical rooms; bar at least half the door width."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Maglock egress: two code paths","where":"Reference","href":"reference/maglock-egress-two-code-paths","text":"Path 1: Sensor release - Egress-side motion sensor unlocks the door; loss of sensor power unlocks - Loss of lock power unlocks - PUSH TO EXIT button 40 to 48 in high, within 5 ft, cuts lock power directly, unlocked at least 30 s - Fire alarm or sprinkler activation unlocks until reset - UL 294 listed Path 2: Door hardware release - Lever or panic bar with a built-in switch that directly cuts lock power - Obvious operation, one hand - Loss of power unlocks - Panic hardware (where required) releases the lock Get AHJ approval first; many restrict maglocks."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Delayed egress","where":"Reference","href":"reference/delayed-egress","text":"- Only in occupancies the code allows, in fully sprinklered or fully detected buildings. - ≤ 15 lbf for ≤ 3 s starts an irreversible release; unlocks within 15 s (30 s if AHJ approves); local alarm sounds. - Rearms manually at the door only. - Releases immediately on sprinkler or detection activation, power loss, and from the fire command center. - Sign within 12 in above the hardware: \"PUSH UNTIL ALARM SOUNDS. DOOR CAN BE OPENED IN 15 SECONDS.\" Letters 1 in high, 1/8 in stroke. - No more than one delayed egress door in the path to an exit."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Fire alarm release","where":"Reference","href":"reference/fire-alarm-release","text":"- Fail-safe locks on egress doors unlock on fire alarm and stay unlocked until reset. - Fire alarm contact in the lock power path (power supply FA input), not a software input. - Fire alarm relay within 3 ft of the controlled device, wiring to the relay supervised. - Stairway doors locked from the stair side unlock for re-entry. - The fire alarm contractor owns the relay; coordinate and test together. - Test every fail-safe door at acceptance and after changes, with the fire alarm account on test."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Fire-rated doors","where":"Reference","href":"reference/fire-rated-doors","text":"- Label on the hinge edge of the door and in the frame. - Must positively latch: electrified hardware listed for fire doors; strikes fail-secure. - No field modification beyond what NFPA 80 allows. - Closer stays; no unlisted hold-opens. - Inspected and tested every year, with records. - Penetrations in rated walls get a listed firestop system."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Door test checklist","where":"Reference","href":"reference/door-test-checklist","text":"- [ ] Valid card: unlocks, relocks, logged with the right name and door - [ ] Invalid and off-schedule card: denied and logged - [ ] REX trips from inside, not from outside - [ ] Forced door and held-open alarms report and restore - [ ] Inside hardware opens the door in every lock state, power off included - [ ] AC off: runs on battery; AC and battery off: fail-safe unlocks, fail-secure stays locked outside - [ ] Fire alarm release (fail-safe egress doors), account on test - [ ] Lock voltage at the lock, energized - [ ] Reader and enclosure tampers report"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Glossary","where":"Reference","href":"reference/glossary","text":"- Access level (access group): a set of doors and schedules given to cardholders. - Anti-passback: a card must exit before it can enter again. - Armature: the steel plate on the door that a maglock holds. - Backset: distance from the door edge to the center of the lock's hub. - Credential: card, fob, phone, PIN, or biometric that identifies a person. - Delayed egress: a lock that delays opening an exit door by 15 or 30 seconds, under strict code rules. - DPS: door position switch; tells the controller the door is closed. - Edge controller: a single-door controller at the door, often powered by PoE. - Electrified hinge: a hinge with wires through it to power hardware in the door. - Facility code: the site number in a Wiegand card number. - Fail-safe: unlocked when power is off. - Fail-secure: locked when power is off. - First-person-in: a scheduled unlock waits for the first valid card. - Forced door: the door opened with no valid card or REX. - Handing: which side the hinges are on and which way the door swings. - Held open: the door stayed open past the allowed time. - Keeper: the part of an electric strike that swings free to release the latch. - Maglock: electromagnetic lock. - MOV: metal oxide varistor; suppresses spikes on AC locks. - OSDP: Open Supervised Device Protocol; encrypted, supervised reader wiring over RS-485. - Preload: door pressure on the latch that can bind a strike. - REX: request to exit. - RS-485: two-wire data bus used by OSDP and many controller networks. - Sensor release: code path for a maglock released by an egress-side motion sensor with a push button backup. - Shunt: ignoring the door contact during a valid entry or exit. - Wiegand: older, unencrypted one-way reader wiring."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Access level (access group)","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a set of doors and schedules given to cardholders."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Anti-passback","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a card must exit before it can enter again."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Armature","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the steel plate on the door that a maglock holds."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Backset","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"distance from the door edge to the center of the lock's hub."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Credential","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"card, fob, phone, PIN, or biometric that identifies a person."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Delayed egress","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a lock that delays opening an exit door by 15 or 30 seconds, under strict code rules."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"DPS","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"door position switch; tells the controller the door is closed."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Edge controller","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a single-door controller at the door, often powered by PoE."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Electrified hinge","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a hinge with wires through it to power hardware in the door."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Facility code","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the site number in a Wiegand card number."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Fail-safe","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"unlocked when power is off."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Fail-secure","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"locked when power is off."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"First-person-in","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a scheduled unlock waits for the first valid card."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Forced door","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the door opened with no valid card or REX."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Handing","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"which side the hinges are on and which way the door swings."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Held open","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the door stayed open past the allowed time."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Keeper","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the part of an electric strike that swings free to release the latch."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Maglock","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"electromagnetic lock."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"MOV","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"metal oxide varistor; suppresses spikes on AC locks."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"OSDP","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"Open Supervised Device Protocol; encrypted, supervised reader wiring over RS-485."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Preload","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"door pressure on the latch that can bind a strike."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"REX","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"request to exit."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"RS-485","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"two-wire data bus used by OSDP and many controller networks."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Sensor release","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"code path for a maglock released by an egress-side motion sensor with a push button backup."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Shunt","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"ignoring the door contact during a valid entry or exit."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Wiegand","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"older, unencrypted one-way reader wiring."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Valid card reads, door doesn't unlock","where":"Guide 1","href":"troubleshoot/1","text":"Symptom: reader beeps and flashes green (or the log shows access granted), but the door stays locked. 1. Check the event log. Does it show granted for that card at that door? No → it's a programming or card issue, go to Guide 3. 2. Listen and feel at the lock while someone badges. Does the strike or lock click? - Clicks but door won't open → mechanical: go to step 6. - No click → electrical: go to step 3. 3. Meter the lock output at the controller or power supply while badging. Voltage switches on (or off, for fail-safe) → the controller side is fine; go to step 4. Nothing changes → check the relay programming (fail-safe vs fail-secure setting), the relay's wiring (NO vs NC contact), and the output fuse or PTC on the power supply. 4. Meter at the lock, energized (Reference: Meter checks at the door ). Low voltage → voltage drop, a loose splice, or a thin power transfer hinge. Use the Voltage Drop calculator . 5. Correct voltage at the lock but no action → lock set for the wrong voltage, wrong fail mode, or a failed coil. Check the jumper and the label. 6. Strike clicks but door won't open: preload. Push the door closed firmly and badge again. Opens → the door is pushing on the keeper (weatherstrip, warped door, closer adjustment, air pressure). Fix the door alignment or use a high-preload strike. 7. Check that the lockset's latch lands in the keeper, and that the deadlatch isn't falling into the keeper gap. Field tip (David to add): the quickest way you prove preload on a strike."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Reader dead: no LED, no beep","where":"Guide 2","href":"troubleshoot/2","text":"Symptom: reader shows no light and doesn't respond to cards. 1. Is the controller online and are other readers on it working? Not → go to Guide 9 (it may be power for the whole panel). 2. Meter reader power at the reader , red to black. About 12 V (or the reader's rating) → go to step 4. Zero → step 3. Low → voltage drop or overload; use the Reader Cable calculator . 3. Meter at the controller's reader terminals. Voltage there but not at the reader → open cable or bad splice. No voltage at the controller → reader power fuse or PTC tripped (often from a short in the reader cable). Disconnect the reader and see if it recovers. 4. Power is good but reader is dead → failed reader, or a reader that needs to be configured (OSDP address or mode). Swap with a known-good reader of the same model. 5. Outdoors: look for water in the back box, corroded terminals, or vandalism."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Card denied, or reads the wrong number","where":"Guide 3","href":"troubleshoot/3","text":"Symptom: a cardholder who should have access is denied, or the log shows a different card number than expected. 1. Read the denial reason in the log: - Unknown card → card not in the system, or the number doesn't match. Go to step 3. - Wrong time / schedule → check the access level's schedule and holidays. - Wrong door / no access → check the cardholder's access levels include this door. - Expired / disabled → check the card's activation and expiration dates. 2. Check the controller's clock and time zone . A controller off by an hour (daylight saving) denies cards at the edges of schedules. 3. Unknown card: compare the number in the log with the number in the cardholder record. Facility code wrong → card enrolled with the wrong format or facility code. Number completely different every read → Wiegand D0 and D1 swapped, or the wrong format set on the reader port. 4. Changes in the software haven't reached the controller → check that the controller is online and the database download finished. 5. One card fails everywhere, others work → damaged card or a card technology the readers don't read."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"False forced-door alarms","where":"Guide 4","href":"troubleshoot/4","text":"Symptom: door forced open alarms with no break-in. 1. Look at the pattern in the log. Forced alarm right after a valid card → the DPS is seeing the door open after the unlock time ended, or relock-on-close isn't set. Forced alarm when people walk out → REX problem. 2. REX: walk up to the door from inside. Does the REX input change state before your hand reaches the hardware? No → re-aim the motion REX toward the door hardware, or use a hardware REX. A REX that trips too late is the most common cause. 3. DPS: open and close the door while watching the input. Bounces open and closed (door rattles, wind, air pressure) → adjust the DPS, increase the gap tolerance, or fix the door. Misaligned magnet → realign. 4. Door doesn't latch: a door that pops open on air pressure or a weak closer shows as forced. Fix the closer and latching. 5. Mechanical key override: someone is using a key. That's correctly reported as forced unless the lock reports key use. 6. Check unlock time: too short for slow users → extend it or give them extended unlock."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Held-open alarms","where":"Guide 5","href":"troubleshoot/5","text":"Symptom: door held open alarms when the door looks closed, or too many held-open alarms. 1. Is the door actually closed and latched? No → closer adjustment, a sweep or threshold dragging, or a propped door (talk to the customer). 2. Door is closed but the DPS reads open → DPS gap too large, magnet missing or misaligned, broken wire. Meter the DPS disconnected: near 0 Ω with the door closed, open with it open (Reference: Meter checks at the door ). 3. Doors that legitimately stay open (deliveries, loading docks) → lengthen the held-open time or put the door on a schedule, with the customer's approval. 4. Pre-alarm beeping without anyone noticing → move the reader beeper or add a local sounder."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Maglock won't hold, or holds weakly","where":"Guide 6","href":"troubleshoot/6","text":"Symptom: door can be pulled open while the maglock should be locked. 1. Check the bond sensor or status LED on the maglock, if it has one. Not bonded → step 2. 2. Voltage at the maglock, energized. Low → voltage drop or wrong voltage setting; a 24 V-set maglock on 12 V holds weakly. 3. Armature: it must float on its rubber washers. Over-tightened, it can't sit flat. Loosen to factory spec. 4. Alignment: the armature should meet the magnet face fully. A door that sags or a bent bracket leaves a gap. Even paper or paint on the faces cuts holding force a lot. 5. Faces: clean rust or debris off both faces; don't grind them. 6. Door opens on REX, push button, or fire alarm when it shouldn't → check those inputs aren't stuck (a failed REX holding the lock released, or a fire alarm relay in alarm)."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Electric strike won't release, or releases intermittently","where":"Guide 7","href":"troubleshoot/7","text":"Symptom: card grants and the strike clicks (or should), but the door stays latched some or all of the time. 1. Preload test: push the door closed hard, then badge. Works only when pushed → preload. Fix the door, adjust the strike position, or use a high-preload strike. 2. Voltage at the strike, energized. Low → voltage drop or a weak power transfer. Correct → step 3. 3. Fit: the latch must sit fully in the keeper pocket with room to move. A latch that rides on the keeper edge, or a deadlatch in the keeper gap, binds. 4. Wrong mode: a fail-safe strike on a fail-secure output (or the reverse) does the opposite of what you expect. 5. Continuous duty: a strike held unlocked for hours on a schedule must be rated for continuous duty; an intermittent strike overheats and fails. 6. Intermittent on cold days → door shrinks or swells; adjust the strike and check the closer."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Door won't relock, or stays unlocked","where":"Guide 8","href":"troubleshoot/8","text":"Symptom: door stays unlocked after the unlock time, or is unlocked when it should be locked. 1. Check the door's mode and schedule in the software. Is it on a scheduled unlock, a manual override, or a lockdown release someone forgot to undo? 2. REX stuck active (motion REX seeing a heater, a sign moving in air, or a door gap) and REX set to unlock → the lock keeps cycling open. Watch the REX input. 3. Fire alarm release still active → the fire alarm panel isn't reset or its relay is stuck. Check with the fire alarm contractor. 4. Fail-safe lock with no power → check the power supply output, fuse, and battery (Guide 11). 5. Relay welded closed on the controller → swap to a spare relay or replace the module, and add a diode at the lock so it doesn't happen again. 6. Strike held mechanically → a latch with a hold-back (dogged exit device) or a taped latch. Not an electrical problem."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Controller offline or communication lost","where":"Guide 9","href":"troubleshoot/9","text":"Symptom: software shows a controller, door module, or reader offline. 1. Is it one controller, a group, or everything? Everything → the server, software service, or network on the server side. 2. One IP controller: does it have power and a link light at its network port? Ping it from a workstation on the same network. No reply → cable, switch port, VLAN change, or an IP address conflict. Ask IT what changed. 3. PoE controller: check the switch's PoE budget and port status. A switch that's out of PoE budget turns off ports. 4. RS-485 door modules: check the bus wiring (A/B polarity, termination at the ends, shield grounded once), addresses (two modules on the same address), and the baud rate. 5. OSDP reader offline: address, baud, and Secure Channel key must match. A reader replaced with a new one needs to be re-keyed. 6. Controller online but doors not working while offline → check that it has its database (some controllers lose it after a power-down with a dead backup battery). Field tip (David to add): the first question you ask IT when a controller drops off."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Doors didn't release on fire alarm (or won't relock after reset)","where":"Guide 10","href":"troubleshoot/10","text":"Symptom: fail-safe doors stayed locked during a fire alarm or a test, or stay unlocked after the fire alarm is reset. This is a life safety failure. Tell the building owner right away, and keep the doors unlocked or posted until it's fixed. 1. Get the fire alarm contractor and put the fire alarm account on test. 2. Activate the release and meter the fire alarm interface input at the access power supply. Does the contact change state? No → the fire alarm relay isn't operating (programming or wiring on the fire alarm side). 3. Contact changes but locks stay powered → the FA input is jumpered, wired to the wrong terminals, or the locks are on an output the FA input doesn't control (a second supply, or the controller's own lock output). Every fail-safe egress lock must lose power on alarm. 4. A push button or REX wired as a bypass around the release → rewire so the fire alarm contact cuts lock power directly. 5. Won't relock after reset: the FA relay is still in alarm or not reset, or the power supply has a latching FA input that needs its own reset. Check the supply's FA input setting. 6. Retest every door the release controls, and record the test."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Power supply troubles: AC loss, low battery, blown output","where":"Guide 11","href":"troubleshoot/11","text":"Symptom: AC fail or low battery reported, or one or more doors lost power. 1. AC fail: check the breaker and the AC input at the supply's terminals. Breaker on but no AC → the circuit or a switched outlet. Label the breaker so it doesn't happen again. 2. Low battery: with AC on, meter the battery. Disconnect it and meter the charger output (often about 13.6 V for a 12 V system). Charger good, battery low after a day of charging → replace the batteries (both in a 24 V pair). Date the new ones. 3. Output dead: find the fuse or PTC for that output. Blown or tripped → disconnect the load and look for a short: a pinched cable at the door, a backward diode, or a failed lock. 4. Overloaded: add up the load with the Access Power calculator . Over about 80% of the rating, or batteries that can't last the required standby → add a supply. 5. Supply cycling or hot → overload or a failing supply."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Controller resets or readers glitch when a lock operates","where":"Guide 12","href":"troubleshoot/12","text":"Symptom: the controller reboots, a reader beeps oddly, or the wrong number reads when a door unlocks or relocks. 1. Does it happen exactly when the lock changes state? Yes → lock kickback or inrush. 2. Check for a suppression diode at the lock (or built-in suppression). Missing → add one at the lock, band to +. 3. Is the lock powered from the same supply or output as the controller or readers? Yes → move locks to a separate supply or output. 4. Solenoid latch retraction exit device → needs a supply made for its inrush. 5. Lock and reader cables bundled together over a long run, with the reader shield not grounded → ground the shield at the controller end and separate the cables where you can. 6. Recheck after the fix by cycling the lock twenty times while watching the controller and reader."}]});
